window.BAHR_DATA=window.BAHR_DATA||{};window.BAHR_DATA["passages-08"]=[{"id":"q2-124","q":"2:124","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:124 · Abraham’s testing and the trust of leadership","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 162–163","cairo":"I:162–163","source_page_first":162,"source_page_last":163,"ar":"وَإِذِ ابْتَلى إِبْراهِيمَ رَبُّهُ بِكَلِماتٍ فَأَتَمَّهُنَّ قالَ إِنِّي جاعِلُكَ لِلنَّاسِ إِماماً قالَ وَمِنْ ذُرِّيَّتِي قالَ لا يَنالُ عَهْدِي الظَّالِمِينَ (124)\nقلت: (ابتلى) اختبر، و (إبراهيم) مفعول، وفيه أربع لغات: إبراهام وإبراهوم وإبراهيم وبالقصر، و (ربه) فاعل، وقدم المفعول للاهتمام، ولئلا يعود الضمير على ما بعده لفظاً ورتبة، و (عهدي) فاعل، و (الظالمين) مفعول.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: واذكر يا محمد، أو اذكروا يا بني إسرائيل، حين اختبر إِبْراهِيمَ رَبُّهُ بِكَلِماتٍ أن يعمل بها، وهي: تسليم بدنه للنيران، وولده للقربان، وطعامه للضَّيفان، أو عشر خصال: خمس في الرأس:\nالمضمضة، والاستنشاق، وقصّ الشارب، والسواك، وفرق الرأس. وقيل: وإعفاء اللحية، وخمس في الجسد: تقليم الظفر، وحلق العانة، ونتف الإبط، والاستنجاء بالماء، والاختتان. أو مناسك الحج أو الخصال التي امتحن بها وهي:\nالكوكب، والقمر، والشمس، والنار. والهجرة، والذبح، والأحسن أنها ثلاث: الهجرة من وطنه، ورمي ولده بمكة، وذبح الآخر حين بلغ أن يسعى معه . فَأَتَمَّهُنَّ أي: وَفَّى بهن، فلما وَفَّى بهن (قال) الله تعالى له: إِنِّي جاعِلُكَ لِلنَّاسِ إِماماً، أي: قدوة بك في التوحيد، أو في الأصول والفروع، إذ لم يبعث بعده نبي إلا كان من ذريته، ومأمور باتباعه.\nولمّا جعله الله إماماً طلب ذلك لأولاده فقال: وَمِنْ ذُرِّيَّتِي فاجعل أئمة، قالَ الحق تعالى: لا يَنالُ عَهْدِي أي: لا يلحق عهدي بالإمامة الظَّالِمِينَ منهم، إذ لا يصلح للإمامة إلا البررة الأتقياء، لأنها أمانة من الله وعهد، والظالم لا يصلح لها، وفيه تنبيه على أنه قد يكون من ذريته ظلمة لا يستحقون الإمامة، وفيه دليل على عصمة الأنبياء قبل البعثة، وأن الفاسق لا يصلح للإمامة. قاله البيضاوي.\nالإشارة: إذا أراد الله تعالى أن يجعل وليّاً من أوليائه إماماً يُقتدى به، وداعياً يدعو إليه، ابتلاه، فإن صبر ورضي اصطفاه، ولحضرته اجتباه، فيكون إماماً يُقتدى به، وداعياً يُهتدى به، وهذه سُنَّة الله تعالى في أصفيائه\nيبتليهم الله تعالى بتسليط الخلق عليهم وأنواع من البلايا، فإذا نقوا من البقايا، وتكلمت فيهم المزايا، أظهرهم للخلق داعين إلى الله ومرشدين إلى طريق الله، وقد تبقى الإمامة فى ذريتهم إن ساروا على هديهم، ومَن لم يسلك به هذا المسلك فلا يصلح للإمامة، وإن توجه إليها كان ناقصّا في الدعوة، ولذلك قال بعضهم: (من ادّعى شهود الجمال قبل تأدُّبه بالجلال، فارفضه فإنه دجال) . هـ. وكل من اتصف بشئ من ظلم العباد لا ينال عهد الإمامة في طريق الإرشاد، وبالله التوفيق، وهو الهادي إلى سواء الطريق.\nثم ذكر شرف البيت الذي هو المقصود، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَإِذِ ابْتَلى إِبْراهِيمَ رَبُّهُ بِكَلِماتٍ فَأَتَمَّهُنَّ قالَ إِنِّي جاعِلُكَ لِلنَّاسِ إِماماً قالَ وَمِنْ ذُرِّيَّتِي قالَ لا يَنالُ عَهْدِي الظَّالِمِينَ (124)","quran_en":"124. When Abraham’s Lord tested him with certain words, and he fulfilled them, He said, “I am making you a leader for people.” Abraham said, “And from my descendants?” He said, “My covenant does not reach the wrongdoers.”","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The test, the agent and the object","html":"<p>I say: to <i>ibtalā<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ابتلى<\/bdi>) is to test. “Abraham” is the object; his name has four forms: Ibrāhām, Ibrāhūm, Ibrāhīm and a shortened form. “His Lord” is the agent. The object precedes for emphasis and to avoid a pronoun referring to something following it both in wording and grammatical rank. “My covenant” is the agent of “reaches,” and “the wrongdoers” its object.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: to ibtalā ( ابتلى ) is to test. “Abraham” is the object; his name has four forms: Ibrāhām, Ibrāhūm, Ibrāhīm and a shortened form. “His Lord” is the agent. The object precedes for emphasis and to avoid a pronoun referring to something following it both in wording and grammatical rank. “My covenant” is the agent of “reaches,” and “the wrongdoers” its object."},{"type":"exposition","title":"The commands Abraham fulfilled","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: remember, Muḥammad—or remember, Children of Israel—when Abraham’s Lord tested him with words to carry out. These were surrendering his body to the fires, his son to sacrifice, and his food to guests. Alternatively, they were ten practices: five concerning the head—rinsing the mouth, drawing water into the nose, trimming the moustache, using the tooth-stick and parting the hair, or, in another account, letting the beard grow—and five concerning the body: clipping the nails, shaving the pubic hair, plucking the armpits, cleansing with water after relieving oneself, and circumcision.<\/p><p>Or they were the rites of pilgrimage; or the trials of the star, moon, sun, fire, migration and sacrifice. The preferable explanation is three trials: migration from his homeland, leaving his son in Mecca, and sacrificing the other when he reached the age to work alongside him.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-84\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B079]<\/a> He fulfilled them all. God then said: I am making you a leader whom people follow in affirming unity, or in both principles and particular ordinances. Every prophet subsequently sent was of his descendants and commanded to follow him.<\/p><p>When God made him a leader, he asked the same for his children: make leaders from my descendants too. The Real said: My covenant of leadership does not reach the wrongdoers among them. Only the righteous and mindful are suited to leadership, for it is a trust and covenant from God. A wrongdoer is not fit for it. The verse indicates that some descendants would be wrongdoers undeserving of leadership. Al-Bayḍāwī says it also proves the prophets’ protection before their mission and the unfitness of a transgressor for leadership.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: remember, Muḥammad—or remember, Children of Israel—when Abraham’s Lord tested him with words to carry out. These were surrendering his body to the fires, his son to sacrifice, and his food to guests. Alternatively, they were ten practices: five concerning the head—rinsing the mouth, drawing water into the nose, trimming the moustache, using the tooth-stick and parting the hair, or, in another account, letting the beard grow—and five concerning the body: clipping the nails, shaving the pubic hair, plucking the armpits, cleansing with water after relieving oneself, and circumcision. Or they were the rites of pilgrimage; or the trials of the star, moon, sun, fire, migration and sacrifice. The preferable explanation is three trials: migration from his homeland, leaving his son in Mecca, and sacrificing the other when he reached the age to work alongside him. [B079] He fulfilled them all. God then said: I am making you a leader whom people follow in affirming unity, or in both principles and particular ordinances. Every prophet subsequently sent was of his descendants and commanded to follow him. When God made him a leader, he asked the same for his children: make leaders from my descendants too. The Real said: My covenant of leadership does not reach the wrongdoers among them. Only the righteous and mindful are suited to leadership, for it is a trust and covenant from God. A wrongdoer is not fit for it. The verse indicates that some descendants would be wrongdoers undeserving of leadership. Al-Bayḍāwī says it also proves the prophets’ protection before their mission and the unfitness of a transgressor for leadership."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Formation through majesty before leadership","html":"<p>When God wishes to make one of His friends a leader to follow and a summoner to Himself, He tests him. If he is patient and content, He chooses and draws him to His presence, making him an exemplar and guide. This is God’s customary way with His chosen:<\/p><p>He tests them by allowing creatures power over them and through various afflictions. When the remnants of self are purified and their excellences speak, He brings them before creation as callers to God and guides upon His way. Leadership may remain in their descendants if they follow their guidance.<\/p><p>Someone not taken through this course is not fit for leadership; should he assume it, his calling is deficient. Hence the saying: “Whoever claims to witness Beauty before being disciplined by Majesty—reject him, for he is an impostor.” Whoever wrongs God’s servants in any way does not obtain the covenant of leadership in spiritual direction. Through God is success; He guides to the level way.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"When God wishes to make one of His friends a leader to follow and a summoner to Himself, He tests him. If he is patient and content, He chooses and draws him to His presence, making him an exemplar and guide. This is God’s customary way with His chosen: He tests them by allowing creatures power over them and through various afflictions. When the remnants of self are purified and their excellences speak, He brings them before creation as callers to God and guides upon His way. Leadership may remain in their descendants if they follow their guidance. Someone not taken through this course is not fit for leadership; should he assume it, his calling is deficient. Hence the saying: “Whoever claims to witness Beauty before being disciplined by Majesty—reject him, for he is an impostor.” Whoever wrongs God’s servants in any way does not obtain the covenant of leadership in spiritual direction. Through God is success; He guides to the level way."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He then mentions the honour of the House, the principal subject here, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then mentions the honour of the House, the principal subject here, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C162.1","page":162,"ar":"قوله: (ورمي ولده بمكة وذبح الآخر)، يفيد أن الذبيح غير الذي ترك بمكة. وإذا كان الذي ترك بمكة هو إسماعيل - كما هو معروف - فإن الذبيح يكون إسحاق. وهذا ما ذهب إليه قلة من العلماء. والراجح أن الذبيح هو إسماعيل عليه السلام، وهذا هو المروي عن جمهرة الصحابة والتابعين - وعليه غالب المحدثين والمفسرين، واستدلوا على ذلك بأدلة كثيرة. انظر: تفسير الرازي وابن كثير، والقول الفسيح في تعيين الذبيح، للسيوطي، والإسرائيليات والموضوعات، للدكتور أبي شهبة.","en":"His words, “leaving his son at Mecca and sacrificing the other,” imply that the son intended for sacrifice was different from the one left at Mecca. Since the one left at Mecca was Ishmael—as is well known—the son intended for sacrifice would be Isaac. A small number of scholars adopted this view. The stronger view is that it was Ishmael, peace be upon him. This is the view transmitted from the majority of the Companions and their successors, and adopted by most traditionists and commentators, who adduce many proofs for it. Consult the commentaries of al-Rāzī and Ibn Kathīr, al-Suyūṭī’s al-Qawl al-fasīḥ fī taʿyīn al-dhabīḥ [title as printed], and Dr Abū Shahba’s al-Isrāʾīliyyāt wa-l-mawḍūʿāt.","anchor":"ورمي ولده بمكة، وذبح الآخر حين بلغ أن يسعى معه"}],"english_characters":3073,"arabic_characters":2228},{"id":"q2-125","q":"2:125","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:125 · The House as return, security and purified heart","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 163–164","cairo":"I:163–164","source_page_first":163,"source_page_last":164,"ar":"وَإِذْ جَعَلْنَا الْبَيْتَ مَثابَةً لِلنَّاسِ وَأَمْناً وَاتَّخِذُوا مِنْ مَقامِ إِبْراهِيمَ مُصَلًّى وَعَهِدْنا إِلى إِبْراهِيمَ وَإِسْماعِيلَ أَنْ طَهِّرا بَيْتِيَ لِلطَّائِفِينَ وَالْعاكِفِينَ وَالرُّكَّعِ السُّجُودِ (125)\nقلت: (المثابة) : المرجع الذي يثُوب الناس إليه كل سنة، و (اتخذوا) : على قراءة الأمر، محكي بقول محذوف، أي: وقلنا اتخذوا، وعلى قراءة الماضي: معطوف على (جعلنا) ، أي: جعلناه مثابة، واتخذه الناس مُصلَّى.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: (و) اذكر يا محمد إِذْ جَعَلْنَا الْبَيْتَ الحرام، أي: الكعبة، مرجعاً للناس يرجعون لزيارته والطواف به كل سنة، وجعلناه محل أمن، كل من دخله كان آمناً من عقوبة الدنيا والآخرة، إما في الدنيا فإنَّ الناس يُتَخطفون من حوله، وأهلُه آمنون، وأما فى الآخرة فلأن الحج يَجُبُّ ما قبله، وهذا يدل على شرف البيت وحرمته.\nوقلنا لهم: اتَّخِذُوا مِنْ مَقامِ إِبْراهِيمَ، وهو الحجر الذي فيه أثر قدميه، مُصَلًّى تصلون إليه، وهو الذي يصلون خلفه ركعتي الطواف، وَعَهِدْنا أي: أوحينا إِلى إِبْراهِيمَ وَإِسْماعِيلَ ولده، بأن قلنا لهما:\nطَهِّرا بَيْتِيَ من الأدناس والأرجاس والأصنام والأوثان، لِلطَّائِفِينَ به وَالْعاكِفِينَ أي: المقيمين فيه، والمصلين فيه الراكعين الساجدين. فكان البيت مطهراً في زمانهما وبعدهما زماناً، ثم أدخلت فيه [الأصنام] فطهّره نبينا محمد صلى الله عليه وسلّم، وتبقى طهارته حتى يأتي أمر الله. والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: القلب هو بيت الرب، يقول الله تبارك وتعالى لبعض أنبيائه: «طهر لي بيتًا أسكنه، فقال: يا رب أي بيت يسعك؟ فقال له: لن تسعني أرضي ولا سمائي، ووسعني قلب عبدي المؤمن» . فإذا تطهر القلب من الأغيار\nوملئ بالأنوار، وتمكنت فيه المعارف والأسرار، كان مرجعاً وملجأ للعباد، كل مَن وصل إليه، وطاف به، كان آمنا من الزيغ والعناد، ومن خواطر السوء وسوء الاعتقاد، ومن دخله بالمحبة والوداد، أَمِن من الطرْد والبعاد، وكان عند الله من أفضل العباد. ومقام إبراهيم- عليه السلام- هو الاستغراق في عين بحر الشهود، ورفع الهمة عن ما سوى الملك المعبود.\nوهذا المقام هو الذي اتخذه العارفون كعبة لصلاة قلوبهم، وغاية لمنتهى قصودهم.\nعِبارَاتُهُم شَتَّى، وحُسْنُكَ واحِدٌ، ... وكُلٌّ إلى ذاك الجَمالِ يُشِيرُ\nوقد عهد الله تعالى إلى أنبيائه وأصفيائه أن يطهروا قلوبهم من الأغيار، ويرفضوا كل ما سواه من الأكدار، لتتهيأ بذلك لطواف الواردات والأنوار، ولعكوف المعارف والأسرار، وتخضع لهيبتها ظواهرُ الأشباح، وتنقاد لجمال بهجتها القلوبُ والأرواح، وما ذلك على الله بعزيز.\nثم ذكر الحق تعالى دعاء إبراهيم الخليل لمكان البيت، زيادة فى تشريفه، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَإِذْ جَعَلْنَا الْبَيْتَ مَثابَةً لِلنَّاسِ وَأَمْناً وَاتَّخِذُوا مِنْ مَقامِ إِبْراهِيمَ مُصَلًّى وَعَهِدْنا إِلى إِبْراهِيمَ وَإِسْماعِيلَ أَنْ طَهِّرا بَيْتِيَ لِلطَّائِفِينَ وَالْعاكِفِينَ وَالرُّكَّعِ السُّجُودِ (125)","quran_en":"125. When We made the House a place of return for people and a sanctuary: “Take Abraham’s station as a place of prayer.” We covenanted with Abraham and Ishmael: “Purify My House for those who circle it, those who dwell in devotion, and those who bow and prostrate.”","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"A place to which people return","html":"<p>I say: <i>mathāba<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مثابة<\/bdi>) is a place of return to which people come back every year. On the imperative reading, “take” is reported after an omitted “We said.” On the past-tense reading it is conjoined to “We made”: We made it a place of return, and people took it as a place of prayer.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: mathāba ( مثابة ) is a place of return to which people come back every year. On the imperative reading, “take” is reported after an omitted “We said.” On the past-tense reading it is conjoined to “We made”: We made it a place of return, and people took it as a place of prayer."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Purification of the Sacred House","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: remember, Muḥammad, when We made the Sacred House—the Kaʿba—a place to which people return yearly for visitation and circumambulation. We made it a place of security: whoever enters is safe from punishment in this world and the next. Here, people are snatched away around it while its inhabitants are secure; hereafter, pilgrimage erases what preceded it. This indicates the House’s honour and sanctity.<\/p><p>We told them to take Abraham’s station—the stone bearing his footprints—as a place towards which to pray, behind which they perform the two units after circumambulation. We covenanted with Abraham and his son Ishmael, revealing to them: purify My House of dirt, defilement, idols and images for those who circle it, reside in it and pray there, bowing and prostrating. It was purified in their time and for a period afterwards. Then idols were brought in, until our Prophet Muḥammad purified it. Its purity remains until God’s command comes. And God Most High knows best.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: remember, Muḥammad, when We made the Sacred House—the Kaʿba—a place to which people return yearly for visitation and circumambulation. We made it a place of security: whoever enters is safe from punishment in this world and the next. Here, people are snatched away around it while its inhabitants are secure; hereafter, pilgrimage erases what preceded it. This indicates the House’s honour and sanctity. We told them to take Abraham’s station—the stone bearing his footprints—as a place towards which to pray, behind which they perform the two units after circumambulation. We covenanted with Abraham and his son Ishmael, revealing to them: purify My House of dirt, defilement, idols and images for those who circle it, reside in it and pray there, bowing and prostrating. It was purified in their time and for a period afterwards. Then idols were brought in, until our Prophet Muḥammad purified it. Its purity remains until God’s command comes. And God Most High knows best."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The heart as the Lord’s House","html":"<p>The heart is the Lord’s House. God Most High says to one of His prophets: “Purify a house for Me to dwell in.” He asks, “Lord, what house can contain You?” God replies, “Neither My earth nor My heaven contains Me, but the heart of My believing servant contains Me.”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-85\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B080]<\/a> When the heart is purified of others,<\/p><p>filled with lights and firmly established in knowledge and mysteries, it becomes a refuge and place of return for servants. Whoever reaches and circles it is safe from deviation and resistance, evil suggestions and wrong belief. Whoever enters it through love and affection is safe from rejection and distance, and is among the best servants with God.<\/p><p>Abraham’s station is absorption in the very sea of witnessing and the lifting of aspiration above everything but the worshipped King. The gnostics take this station as the Kaʿba for their hearts’ prayer and the final goal of their purposes:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">Their expressions differ, but Your beauty is one;<br>each points towards that Beauty.<\/blockquote><p>God covenants with His prophets and chosen ones to purify their hearts of others and discard every impurity of other-than-Him, preparing them for the circling of inspirations and lights, the abiding of knowledge and mysteries. Outward bodies bow before their awe; hearts and spirits submit to their radiant beauty. That is not difficult for God.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"The heart is the Lord’s House. God Most High says to one of His prophets: “Purify a house for Me to dwell in.” He asks, “Lord, what house can contain You?” God replies, “Neither My earth nor My heaven contains Me, but the heart of My believing servant contains Me.” [B080] When the heart is purified of others, filled with lights and firmly established in knowledge and mysteries, it becomes a refuge and place of return for servants. Whoever reaches and circles it is safe from deviation and resistance, evil suggestions and wrong belief. Whoever enters it through love and affection is safe from rejection and distance, and is among the best servants with God. Abraham’s station is absorption in the very sea of witnessing and the lifting of aspiration above everything but the worshipped King. The gnostics take this station as the Kaʿba for their hearts’ prayer and the final goal of their purposes: Their expressions differ, but Your beauty is one; each points towards that Beauty. God covenants with His prophets and chosen ones to purify their hearts of others and discard every impurity of other-than-Him, preparing them for the circling of inspirations and lights, the abiding of knowledge and mysteries. Outward bodies bow before their awe; hearts and spirits submit to their radiant beauty. That is not difficult for God."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real recounts Abraham the Intimate’s prayer for the place of the House, further honouring it:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real recounts Abraham the Intimate’s prayer for the place of the House, further honouring it:"}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C163.1","page":163,"ar":"ما بين المعكوفتين زيادة ليست في الأصول.","en":"The words between square brackets are an addition absent from the source copies.","anchor":"[الأصنام]"}],"english_characters":2729,"arabic_characters":2256},{"id":"q2-126","q":"2:126","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:126 · Provision for believers and unbelievers","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 164–165","cairo":"I:164–165","source_page_first":164,"source_page_last":165,"ar":"وَإِذْ قالَ إِبْراهِيمُ رَبِّ اجْعَلْ هذا بَلَداً آمِناً وَارْزُقْ أَهْلَهُ مِنَ الثَّمَراتِ مَنْ آمَنَ مِنْهُمْ بِاللَّهِ وَالْيَوْمِ الْآخِرِ قالَ وَمَنْ كَفَرَ فَأُمَتِّعُهُ قَلِيلاً ثُمَّ أَضْطَرُّهُ إِلى عَذابِ النَّارِ وَبِئْسَ الْمَصِيرُ (126)\nقلت: الإشارة تعود إلى المكان، أو البلد، أي: اجعل هذا المكان بلداً ذا أمن، قال بعضهم: نكَّرَ البلد هنا، وعرَّفه في سورة إبراهيم، لان هذا الدعاء وقع قبل أن يكون بلداً، وفي سورة إبراهيم وقع بعد أن كان بلداً فلذلك عرَّفه، وفيه نظر من جهة التاريخ، وسيأتي تمامه هناك إن شاء الله.\nوقوله: (مَن آمَن) : بدل من (أهله) ، بدل البعض للتخصيص، و (مَن كفر) : معطوف على (مَن آمَن) ، على حذف المضارع، أي: وارزق من كفر.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَاذكر إِذْ قالَ إِبْراهِيمُ في دعائه لمكة لما أنزَل ابنَه بها بوادٍ غير ذي زرع، وتركه في يد الله تعالى: رَبِّ اجْعَلْ هذا المكان بَلَداً آمِناً يأمن فيه كل من يأوي إليه، وَارْزُقْ أَهْلَهُ مِنَ أنواع الثَّمَراتِ، كالحبوب وسائر الفواكه، مَنْ آمَنَ مِنْهُمْ بِاللَّهِ وَالْيَوْمِ الْآخِرِ قالَ الحق جلّ جلاله: بل وأرزق أيضاً مَنْ كَفَرَ في الدنيا، فَأُمَتِّعُهُ زمناً قَلِيلًا، أو تمتيعاً قليلاً. ثُمَّ ألجئه إِلى عَذابِ النَّارِ وبئس المرجع مصيره.\nقاس إبراهيم الخليل الرزق على الإمامة، فنبَّه سبحانه وتعالى أن الرزق رحمة دنيوية، تعم المؤمن والكافر، بخلاف الإمامة، والتقدم في الدين، فإنها سبب النعيم الأخروي، ولا ينالها إلا أهلُ الإيمان والصلاح.\nالإشارة: دعاء الأنبياء عليهم السلام، كما يصدقُ بالحس يصدق بالمعنى، فيشمل دعاء الخليل القلوبَ التي هي بلد الإيمان، والأرواح التي هي معدن الأسرار والإحسان، فتكون آمنة من طوارق الشيطان، ومحفوظة من الوقوف مع رؤية الأكوان، آمنة من الأكدار، محفوظة من رؤية الأغيار، فيرزقها الله من ثمرة العلوم، ويفتح لها من مخازن الفهوم، من آمن منهم بالشريعة الظاهرة، وجاهد نفسه في عمل الطريقة الباطنة، حتى أشرقت عليه أنوار الحقيقة العيانية، وأما من كفر بطريق الخصوص، ووقف مع ظواهر النصوص، فإنما يُمَتَّع بعلم الرسوم الذي حدُّ حلاوته اللسان، ثم يلجأ إلى عذاب الحجاب، وسوء الحساب، ولم يُفْضِ إلى حلاوة الشهود والعيان، التي يمتع بها الجَنَانُ حتى يُفْضي إلى نعيم الجنَان، فيتم النعيم بالنظر إلى وجهه الكريم، منحنا الله من ذلك حظا وافرا بمنِّه وكرمه.\nثم ذكر الحق تعالى كيفية بناء البيت، وما كان شعارهما فى حالة بنائه، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَإِذْ قالَ إِبْراهِيمُ رَبِّ اجْعَلْ هذا بَلَداً آمِناً وَارْزُقْ أَهْلَهُ مِنَ الثَّمَراتِ مَنْ آمَنَ مِنْهُمْ بِاللَّهِ وَالْيَوْمِ الْآخِرِ قالَ وَمَنْ كَفَرَ فَأُمَتِّعُهُ قَلِيلاً ثُمَّ أَضْطَرُّهُ إِلى عَذابِ النَّارِ وَبِئْسَ الْمَصِيرُ (126)","quran_en":"126. When Abraham said, “My Lord, make this a secure town and provide its people with fruits—those among them who believe in God and the Last Day,” He said, “And whoever disbelieves, I shall give him brief enjoyment, then compel him to the punishment of the Fire. An evil destination!”","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The town and the particularization of its people","html":"<p>I say: the demonstrative refers to the place or town: make this place a town possessing security. Someone explains that “town” is indefinite here but definite in Sūrat Ibrāhīm [14:35], because this prayer preceded its becoming a town, whereas that prayer followed it. This raises a chronological difficulty; its fuller discussion will come there, God willing.<\/p><p>“Those who believe” is an apposition of the part to “its people,” particularizing them. “Whoever disbelieves” is conjoined to “those who believe,” with an omitted imperfect: “and I shall provide for whoever disbelieves.”<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: the demonstrative refers to the place or town: make this place a town possessing security. Someone explains that “town” is indefinite here but definite in Sūrat Ibrāhīm [14:35], because this prayer preceded its becoming a town, whereas that prayer followed it. This raises a chronological difficulty; its fuller discussion will come there, God willing. “Those who believe” is an apposition of the part to “its people,” particularizing them. “Whoever disbelieves” is conjoined to “those who believe,” with an omitted imperfect: “and I shall provide for whoever disbelieves.”"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Provision differs from religious leadership","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: remember Abraham’s prayer for Mecca after settling his son there in an uncultivated valley and leaving him in God’s care. “My Lord, make this place a secure town, where whoever takes shelter is safe. Provide its inhabitants with fruits of every kind, grain and all other produce—those believing in God and the Last Day.” The Real says: I also provide for the unbeliever in this world, granting him a brief time or scant measure of enjoyment, then compelling him to the Fire. What an evil place to return!<\/p><p>Abraham the Intimate compared provision with leadership. God teaches that provision is a worldly mercy encompassing believer and unbeliever, unlike leadership and precedence in religion, which occasion heavenly bliss and are attained only by people of faith and righteousness.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: remember Abraham’s prayer for Mecca after settling his son there in an uncultivated valley and leaving him in God’s care. “My Lord, make this place a secure town, where whoever takes shelter is safe. Provide its inhabitants with fruits of every kind, grain and all other produce—those believing in God and the Last Day.” The Real says: I also provide for the unbeliever in this world, granting him a brief time or scant measure of enjoyment, then compelling him to the Fire. What an evil place to return! Abraham the Intimate compared provision with leadership. God teaches that provision is a worldly mercy encompassing believer and unbeliever, unlike leadership and precedence in religion, which occasion heavenly bliss and are attained only by people of faith and righteousness."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The fruits of the sciences","html":"<p>The prophets’ prayers apply to inward meanings as to sensible realities. Abraham’s prayer thus includes hearts, the towns of faith, and spirits, the mines of mysteries and excellence. They become safe from Satan’s assaults and protected from stopping at the sight of created things; secure from impurities, protected from seeing others. God gives them the fruits of the sciences and opens the treasuries of understanding: those who believe the outward Law and struggle in the inward practice of the Path until the lights of directly witnessed Reality shine upon them.<\/p><p>But someone who denies the special path and stops at the outward texts enjoys only formal learning (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">علم الرسوم<\/bdi>), whose sweetness extends no further than the tongue. He is then driven to the punishment of veiling and an evil reckoning. He has not reached the sweetness of witnessing and direct vision that delights the heart (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الجَنان<\/bdi>) until it reaches the bliss of the gardens (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الجِنان<\/bdi>), perfected by looking upon His noble Face. May God grant us an abundant share through His favour and generosity.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"The prophets’ prayers apply to inward meanings as to sensible realities. Abraham’s prayer thus includes hearts, the towns of faith, and spirits, the mines of mysteries and excellence. They become safe from Satan’s assaults and protected from stopping at the sight of created things; secure from impurities, protected from seeing others. God gives them the fruits of the sciences and opens the treasuries of understanding: those who believe the outward Law and struggle in the inward practice of the Path until the lights of directly witnessed Reality shine upon them. But someone who denies the special path and stops at the outward texts enjoys only formal learning ( علم الرسوم ), whose sweetness extends no further than the tongue. He is then driven to the punishment of veiling and an evil reckoning. He has not reached the sweetness of witnessing and direct vision that delights the heart ( الجَنان ) until it reaches the bliss of the gardens ( الجِنان ), perfected by looking upon His noble Face. May God grant us an abundant share through His favour and generosity."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real describes the House’s construction and what its builders uttered while building:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real describes the House’s construction and what its builders uttered while building:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":2563,"arabic_characters":2115},{"id":"q2-127","q":"2:127","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:127 · Raising the foundations of surrender, faith and excellence","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 165–166","cairo":"I:165–166","source_page_first":165,"source_page_last":166,"ar":"وَإِذْ يَرْفَعُ إِبْراهِيمُ الْقَواعِدَ مِنَ الْبَيْتِ وَإِسْماعِيلُ رَبَّنا تَقَبَّلْ مِنَّا إِنَّكَ أَنْتَ السَّمِيعُ الْعَلِيمُ (127)\nقلت: (القواعد) جمع قاعدة، وهي الأساس، وكأنه مأخوذ من القعود بمعنى الثبات، وأما القواعد من النساء، فجمع قاعد، بلا تاء، لأنه وصف خاص بالنساء، فلا يحتاج إلى تمييز التاء، و (ربنا) منصوب على النداء محكي بحال محذوفة، أي: حال كونهم قائلين ربنا ... الخ.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: واذكر وقت رفع إِبْراهِيمُ الْقَواعِدَ مِنَ الْبَيْتِ وَإِسْماعِيلُ، وبنائهما له، بعد أن درس بالطوفان، وكان بناء آدم عليه السلام لما أُهبط إلى الأرض بإعلام الملائكة. كان إبراهيم عليه السلام يبني، وإسماعيلُ يُناوله الحجارة، فنسب البناء لهما لتعاونهما، وقيل: كانا يبنيان كُلٍّ في ناحية، حال كونهما قَائِلَيْنِ:\nرَبَّنا تَقَبَّلْ مِنَّا عملنا هذا، إِنَّكَ أَنْتَ السَّمِيعُ لدعائنا، الْعَلِيمُ بنياتنا وسرائرنا.\nالإشارة: ينبغي للعبد أن يرفع قواعد إسلامه، ويشيد دعائمه بتحقيق أركانه، كإتقان الشهادتين بتحقيق معانيها، وإتقان الصلاة بإتقان أركانها الظاهرة والباطنة، وإتقان الزكاة بإخلاص أدائها، وإتقان الصيام بتحصيل آدابه، وإتقان الحج بتحصيل مناسكه بعد وجوبه، ويرفع أيضاً قواعد إيمانه بتحقيق أركانه، وهي: الإيمان بالله وملائكته وكتبه ورسله واليوم الآخر، وبالقدر خيره وشره، حُلْوِه ومُره، اعتقاداً وذوقاً، ويرفع أيضاً قواعد إحسانه،\nبتحصيل مراتبه، كتحقيق المشاهدة، وهو أن يعبد الله كأنه يراه، فإن لم يستطع فليعبده كأن الله يراه، وإن شئت قلت: رفعُ قواعد الإسلام يكون بتحقيق التوبة والتقوى والاستقامة، ورفع قواعد الإيمان يكون بتحقيق الإخلاص والصدق والطمأنينة، ورفع قواعد الإحسان يكون بالمراقبة والمشاهدة والمعرفة، كما قال الساحلى- رحمه الله-.\nثم ذكر الحق تعالى دعاءهم بعد البناء، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَإِذْ يَرْفَعُ إِبْراهِيمُ الْقَواعِدَ مِنَ الْبَيْتِ وَإِسْماعِيلُ رَبَّنا تَقَبَّلْ مِنَّا إِنَّكَ أَنْتَ السَّمِيعُ الْعَلِيمُ (127)","quran_en":"127. When Abraham and Ishmael raised the foundations of the House: “Our Lord, accept this from us. You are the All-Hearing, the All-Knowing.”","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Foundations and the omitted circumstantial expression","html":"<p>I say: <i>qawāʿid<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">قواعد<\/bdi>) is the plural of <i>qāʿida<\/i>, a foundation, apparently derived from sitting in the sense of stability. By contrast, <i>qawāʿid<\/i> said of women [24:60] is the plural of <i>qāʿid<\/i> without a feminine ending: the description is particular to women and needs no tāʾ to distinguish the gender. “Our Lord” is accusative as a vocative, reported after an omitted circumstantial phrase: “while saying, ‘Our Lord…’”.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: qawāʿid ( قواعد ) is the plural of qāʿida , a foundation, apparently derived from sitting in the sense of stability. By contrast, qawāʿid said of women [24:60] is the plural of qāʿid without a feminine ending: the description is particular to women and needs no tāʾ to distinguish the gender. “Our Lord” is accusative as a vocative, reported after an omitted circumstantial phrase: “while saying, ‘Our Lord…’”."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Building and praying for acceptance","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: remember when Abraham and Ishmael raised the House’s foundations, rebuilding it after the Flood had effaced it. Adam had built it when sent down to earth, instructed by the angels. Abraham built while Ishmael handed him stones; their cooperation permits attributing the construction to both. Alternatively, each built a different side. They said: our Lord, accept this deed from us; You hear our prayer and know our intentions and inward secrets.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: remember when Abraham and Ishmael raised the House’s foundations, rebuilding it after the Flood had effaced it. Adam had built it when sent down to earth, instructed by the angels. Abraham built while Ishmael handed him stones; their cooperation permits attributing the construction to both. Alternatively, each built a different side. They said: our Lord, accept this deed from us; You hear our prayer and know our intentions and inward secrets."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The three sets of foundations","html":"<p>The servant should raise the foundations of his surrender and strengthen its supports by realizing its pillars: perfect the two testimonies by realizing their meanings; perfect prayer through its outward and inward pillars; perfect almsgiving through sincere payment; fasting through its courtesies; and pilgrimage, when obligatory, through its rites. He should also raise faith’s foundations by realizing its pillars: belief in God, His angels, Books, messengers, the Last Day and the decree, good and evil, sweet and bitter—in conviction and in taste. He should likewise raise the foundations of spiritual excellence,<\/p><p>attaining its degrees: realize witnessing, worshipping God as though seeing Him; if unable, worship as though God sees him.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-86\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B081]<\/a><\/p><p>Or say: surrender’s foundations are raised through repentance, mindfulness and uprightness; faith’s through sincerity, truthfulness and tranquillity; excellence’s through watchfulness, witnessing and gnosis. Thus al-Sāḥilī, God have mercy on him.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"The servant should raise the foundations of his surrender and strengthen its supports by realizing its pillars: perfect the two testimonies by realizing their meanings; perfect prayer through its outward and inward pillars; perfect almsgiving through sincere payment; fasting through its courtesies; and pilgrimage, when obligatory, through its rites. He should also raise faith’s foundations by realizing its pillars: belief in God, His angels, Books, messengers, the Last Day and the decree, good and evil, sweet and bitter—in conviction and in taste. He should likewise raise the foundations of spiritual excellence, attaining its degrees: realize witnessing, worshipping God as though seeing Him; if unable, worship as though God sees him. [B081] Or say: surrender’s foundations are raised through repentance, mindfulness and uprightness; faith’s through sincerity, truthfulness and tranquillity; excellence’s through watchfulness, witnessing and gnosis. Thus al-Sāḥilī, God have mercy on him."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real recounts their prayer after the building:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real recounts their prayer after the building:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":1951,"arabic_characters":1578},{"id":"q2-128-129","q":"2:128–129","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:128–129 · The prayer for surrender, rites and a purifying messenger","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 166–167","cairo":"I:166–167","source_page_first":166,"source_page_last":167,"ar":"رَبَّنا وَاجْعَلْنا مُسْلِمَيْنِ لَكَ وَمِنْ ذُرِّيَّتِنا أُمَّةً مُسْلِمَةً لَكَ وَأَرِنا مَناسِكَنا وَتُبْ عَلَيْنا إِنَّكَ أَنْتَ التَّوَّابُ الرَّحِيمُ (128) رَبَّنا وَابْعَثْ فِيهِمْ رَسُولاً مِنْهُمْ يَتْلُوا عَلَيْهِمْ آياتِكَ وَيُعَلِّمُهُمُ الْكِتابَ وَالْحِكْمَةَ وَيُزَكِّيهِمْ إِنَّكَ أَنْتَ الْعَزِيزُ الْحَكِيمُ (129)\nقلت: قال ابن عباس رضي الله عنه: لما فرغ إبراهيم وإسماعيل من بناء البيت، دَعَوَا بهذا الدعاء، فقالا: (ربنا واجعلنا مسلمين لك) أي: منقادين لأوامرك الظاهرة ولأحكامك القهرية.\nواجعل مِنْ ذُرِّيَّتِنا أُمَّةً أي: جماعة مُسْلِمَةً لَكَ. عَلِمَا- بوحي أو إلهام- أنه يكون من ذريتهما من يكفر بالله، وَأَرِنا أي: عرفنا وعلمنا مَناسِكَنا في الحج. والنُّسُك في الأصل: غاية العبادة، وشاع في الحج لما فيه من المشاق والكلفة، والبعد عن العادة. وَتُبْ عَلَيْنا مما لا يليق بحالنا، فحسنات الأبرار سيئات المقربين، فلكل مقام ما ينقصه وإن كان كاملاً. ولهذا كان عليه الصلاة والسلام يستغفر في المجلس سبعين مرة. إذ ما من مقام إلا وقبله ما فيه نقص، فإذا ترقى عنه استغفر منه، إِنَّكَ أَنْتَ التَّوَّابُ الرَّحِيمُ أي: كثير القبول والإقبال على التائبين.\nرَبَّنا وَابْعَثْ فِيهِمْ أي: في الذرية رَسُولًا مِنْهُمْ وهو مولانا محمد صلى الله عليه وسلّم قال- عليه الصلاة والسلام-:\n«أنا دعوة أبي إبراهيم، وبشارة عيسى» ، حال كونه يَتْلُوا عَلَيْهِمْ أي: يبلغهم آياتِكَ الدالة على توحيدك وصدق رسالتك، وَيُعَلِّمُهُمُ الْكِتابَ أي: القرآن وَالْحِكْمَةَ أي: الشريعة أو السنة. وقال مالك: هي الفقه في الدين والفهم فيه، أو نور يضعه في قلب من شاء من عباده، وَيُزَكِّيهِمْ أي: يطهرهم من لوث المعاصي وكدر الحس، إِنَّكَ أَنْتَ الْعَزِيزُ الغالب في حكمه وسلطانه، الْحَكِيمُ في صنعه وإتقانه، والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: تضمن دعاؤهما عليهما السلام ثلاثة أمور يُطلب التماسها والتحقق بها من كل أحد أولها:\nالانقياد لله في الظاهر والباطن، بامتثال أمره والاستسلام لقهره، حتى يسري ذلك من الأصل إلى فرعه، وهي غاية المِنَّة، قال في الحكم: «متى جعلك في الظاهر ممتثلاً لأمره، وفي الباطن مستسلماً لقهره، فقد أعظم منته\nعليك» . والثاني: معرفة الطريق، والسلوك على جادتها، كارتكاب مشاق الطاعات، ومعانقة مخالفة الهوى والشهوات، ورؤية التقصير في ذلك، وطلب التوبة مما هنالك، وهذه هي مناسك حج القلوب، والطريق الموصل إلى عَرَفَةِ حَضْرَةِ الغيوب، والثالث: الظفر بالداعي إلى الله والدال عليه، وهو المعلم الأكبر، صحبته تطهر من العيوب، ورؤيته تغني القلوب، وتدخلها إلى حضرة الغيوب، ظاهره قائم بوظائف الحكمة، وباطنه مشاهد لتصاريف القدرة، وهذا هو القائم بالتربية النبوية. وبالله التوفيق.\nولما قرر شرف إبراهيم عليه السّلام وجعله إماما يقتدى به، حذر من ترك دينه والرغبة عن ملته، فقال:","quran_ar":"رَبَّنا وَاجْعَلْنا مُسْلِمَيْنِ لَكَ وَمِنْ ذُرِّيَّتِنا أُمَّةً مُسْلِمَةً لَكَ وَأَرِنا مَناسِكَنا وَتُبْ عَلَيْنا إِنَّكَ أَنْتَ التَّوَّابُ الرَّحِيمُ (128) رَبَّنا وَابْعَثْ فِيهِمْ رَسُولاً مِنْهُمْ يَتْلُوا عَلَيْهِمْ آياتِكَ وَيُعَلِّمُهُمُ الْكِتابَ وَالْحِكْمَةَ وَيُزَكِّيهِمْ إِنَّكَ أَنْتَ الْعَزِيزُ الْحَكِيمُ (129)","quran_en":"128. Our Lord, make us both surrendered to You, and from our descendants a community surrendered to You. Show us our rites and turn towards us in mercy. You are the Ever-Returning, the Merciful. 129. Our Lord, raise among them a messenger from themselves, reciting Your signs to them, teaching them the Book and wisdom, and purifying them. You are the Mighty, the Wise.","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Surrender and repentance appropriate to one’s station","html":"<p>I say: Ibn ʿAbbās relates that when Abraham and Ishmael finished building the House, they prayed: our Lord, make us surrendered to Your outward commands and Your overpowering decrees. Make from our descendants a community surrendered to You. Through revelation or inspiration they knew that some descendants would disbelieve. Show us—that is, acquaint us with and teach us—our pilgrimage rites. <i>Nusuk<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">نسك<\/bdi>) originally means the utmost in worship; it became common for pilgrimage because of its hardships, burdens and distance from ordinary habits.<\/p><p>Turn towards us in mercy for what is unfitting to our state. The good deeds of the righteous are the shortcomings of those brought near: every station has something that diminishes it, however complete. Hence he, peace and blessings be upon him, asked forgiveness seventy times in a gathering. No station lacks a preceding one containing deficiency; upon rising from it, he sought forgiveness for it. You are the Ever-Returning, the Merciful, abundant in acceptance and turning towards the repentant.<\/p><p>Our Lord, raise among the descendants a messenger of their own—our master Muḥammad, who said: “I am the prayer of my father Abraham and the good tidings of Jesus.” He will recite and convey Your signs of unity and the truth of Your messengership, teaching them the Book—the Qurʾān—and wisdom: the Sacred Law or Sunna. Mālik says wisdom is understanding and discernment in religion, or a light God places in the heart of whichever servant He wills. He purifies them of the stain of disobedience and the turbidity of sensible fixation. You are Mighty, prevailing in judgement and dominion, Wise in fashioning and perfecting. And God Most High knows best.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: Ibn ʿAbbās relates that when Abraham and Ishmael finished building the House, they prayed: our Lord, make us surrendered to Your outward commands and Your overpowering decrees. Make from our descendants a community surrendered to You. Through revelation or inspiration they knew that some descendants would disbelieve. Show us—that is, acquaint us with and teach us—our pilgrimage rites. Nusuk ( نسك ) originally means the utmost in worship; it became common for pilgrimage because of its hardships, burdens and distance from ordinary habits. Turn towards us in mercy for what is unfitting to our state. The good deeds of the righteous are the shortcomings of those brought near: every station has something that diminishes it, however complete. Hence he, peace and blessings be upon him, asked forgiveness seventy times in a gathering. No station lacks a preceding one containing deficiency; upon rising from it, he sought forgiveness for it. You are the Ever-Returning, the Merciful, abundant in acceptance and turning towards the repentant. Our Lord, raise among the descendants a messenger of their own—our master Muḥammad, who said: “I am the prayer of my father Abraham and the good tidings of Jesus.” He will recite and convey Your signs of unity and the truth of Your messengership, teaching them the Book—the Qurʾān—and wisdom: the Sacred Law or Sunna. Mālik says wisdom is understanding and discernment in religion, or a light God places in the heart of whichever servant He wills. He purifies them of the stain of disobedience and the turbidity of sensible fixation. You are Mighty, prevailing in judgement and dominion, Wise in fashioning and perfecting. And God Most High knows best."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Three things to seek and realize","html":"<p>Their prayer contains three things everyone should seek and realize. First is outward and inward surrender to God: obeying His command and yielding to His irresistible decree, until it passes from the root to its branches. This is the utmost favour. In the <i>Ḥikam<\/i>: “When He makes you outwardly obedient to His command and inwardly surrendered to His overpowering decree, He has bestowed an immense favour<\/p><p>upon you.” Second is knowing the way and travelling its clear course: undertaking the hardships of obedience, embracing opposition to desire and appetite, seeing one’s shortcomings and seeking repentance for them. These are the rites of the hearts’ pilgrimage, the road to the ʿArafa of the unseen presence.<\/p><p>Third is finding one who summons to God and points to Him: the great teacher, whose companionship purifies faults, whose sight enriches hearts and brings them into the unseen presence. Outwardly he fulfils the duties of wisdom; inwardly he witnesses the dispositions of power. He is the one undertaking Prophetic formation. Through God is success.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Their prayer contains three things everyone should seek and realize. First is outward and inward surrender to God: obeying His command and yielding to His irresistible decree, until it passes from the root to its branches. This is the utmost favour. In the Ḥikam : “When He makes you outwardly obedient to His command and inwardly surrendered to His overpowering decree, He has bestowed an immense favour upon you.” Second is knowing the way and travelling its clear course: undertaking the hardships of obedience, embracing opposition to desire and appetite, seeing one’s shortcomings and seeking repentance for them. These are the rites of the hearts’ pilgrimage, the road to the ʿArafa of the unseen presence. Third is finding one who summons to God and points to Him: the great teacher, whose companionship purifies faults, whose sight enriches hearts and brings them into the unseen presence. Outwardly he fulfils the duties of wisdom; inwardly he witnesses the dispositions of power. He is the one undertaking Prophetic formation. Through God is success."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>Having established Abraham’s honour and made him a leader to follow, the Real warns against abandoning his religion and turning from his way:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Having established Abraham’s honour and made him a leader to follow, the Real warns against abandoning his religion and turning from his way:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":2904,"arabic_characters":2437},{"id":"q2-130-132","q":"2:130–132","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:130–132 · Abraham’s religion and the testament of surrender","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 167–168","cairo":"I:167–168","source_page_first":167,"source_page_last":168,"ar":"وَمَنْ يَرْغَبُ عَنْ مِلَّةِ إِبْراهِيمَ إِلاَّ مَنْ سَفِهَ نَفْسَهُ وَلَقَدِ اصْطَفَيْناهُ فِي الدُّنْيا وَإِنَّهُ فِي الْآخِرَةِ لَمِنَ الصَّالِحِينَ (130) إِذْ قالَ لَهُ رَبُّهُ أَسْلِمْ قالَ أَسْلَمْتُ لِرَبِّ الْعالَمِينَ (131) وَوَصَّى بِها إِبْراهِيمُ بَنِيهِ وَيَعْقُوبُ يا بَنِيَّ إِنَّ اللَّهَ اصْطَفى لَكُمُ الدِّينَ فَلا تَمُوتُنَّ إِلاَّ وَأَنْتُمْ مُسْلِمُونَ (132)\nقلت: (من) : استفهامية إنكارية، فيها معنى النفي، مبتدأ، و (يرغب) وما بعده خبر، و (إلا) إبطال لنفيها الذي تضمنته، و (مَن سَفِه) بدل من ضمير (يرغب) على المختار، و (نفسه) مفعول «سَفِه» لتضمنه معنى جهل أو أهلك، قاله الزجاج، أو على التمييز قاله الفراء لأن الضمير فيه معنى الشيوع الذي في (مَن) فلم يكسب التعريف، أو على إسقاط الجارّ وإيصال الفعل إليه، كقولهم: ضرب فلان الظهر والبطن. و (إذ) معمول لاصطفيناه، وأوصى ووصى: لغتان، إلا أن وصى فيه معنى التكثير. وضمير (بها) يعود على كلمة (أسلمت) ، أو الملة، و (يعقوب) معطوف على «إبراهيم» ، و (بني) محكي بحال محذوفة، أي: قائلين يا بني، أو مبتدأ، والخبر محذوف، أي: قال يا بني ... الخ، فيوقف على (بنيه) .\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَمَنْ هذا الذي يَرْغَبُ عَنْ مِلَّةِ إِبْراهِيمَ الواضحة إِلَّا من جهل قدر نَفْسَهُ وبخسها حقها؟ أو إلا من خف رأيه وسفهت نفسه؟ وكيف يرغب عاقل عنها وقد اخترناه إماماً فِي الدُّنْيا يقتدي به أهل الظاهر والباطن؟ وَإِنَّهُ فِي الْآخِرَةِ لَمِنَ الصَّالِحِينَ لحضرتنا، والساكنين في جوارنا.\nوإنما اخترناه لذلك لأنه حين قالَ لَهُ رَبُّهُ: استسلم لحكمنا، وانقد لأمرنا، قال سريعاً: أَسْلَمْتُ وجهي لِرَبِّ الْعالَمِينَ، وانقدْتُ بكُلّيتي إليه. وَوَصَّى بهذه الكلمة أو الملة إِبْراهِيمُ\n، عند موته، بَنِيهِ، وكانوا أربعة: إسماعيل وإسحاق ومدين ومدان. وكذلك حفيده يَعْقُوبُ أوصى بهذه الكلمة بنيه. وكانوا اثني عشر، على ما يأتي في الأسباط، قائلين في تلك الوصية: يا بَنِيَّ إِنَّ اللَّهَ اختار لكم الدِّينَ الحنيف الواضح المنيف، فتمسكوا به ما عِشْتُّم، ولا تموتن إِلَّا وَأَنْتُمْ مُسْلِمُونَ متمسكون به.\nالإشارة: ملة أبينا إبراهيم عليه السلام هي رفع الهمة عن الخلق، وإفراد الوجهة للملك الحق، ورفض الوسائط والأسباب، والتعلق بربّ الأرباب، وفي ذلك يقول الشاعر، وهو الششتري:\nفَرَفْضُ السّويَ فَرْضٌ علينا لأنَّنا ... بملةِ محْوِ الشّركِ والشَّكِّ قدْ دِنَّا\nومِنْ ملته أيضاً: تركُ التدبير والاختيار، والاستسلام لأحكام الواحد القهار، فمن تمسك بهذه الخصال على التمام، ووصى بها من لقيه من الأنام، جعله الله في الدنيا إماماً يقتدي بأقواله ويهتدي بأنواره، وإنه في الآخرة لمن الصالحين المقربين مع النبيين والمرسلين، وأما من رَغِبَ عن هذه الملة الحنيفية فقد خسر الدنيا والآخرة. نسأل الله الحفظ بمنِّه وكرمه.\nولما ادّعت اليهود أن اليهودية هى ملة إبراهيم عليه السلام كذّبهم الله تعالى، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَمَنْ يَرْغَبُ عَنْ مِلَّةِ إِبْراهِيمَ إِلاَّ مَنْ سَفِهَ نَفْسَهُ وَلَقَدِ اصْطَفَيْناهُ فِي الدُّنْيا وَإِنَّهُ فِي الْآخِرَةِ لَمِنَ الصَّالِحِينَ (130) إِذْ قالَ لَهُ رَبُّهُ أَسْلِمْ قالَ أَسْلَمْتُ لِرَبِّ الْعالَمِينَ (131) وَوَصَّى بِها إِبْراهِيمُ بَنِيهِ وَيَعْقُوبُ يا بَنِيَّ إِنَّ اللَّهَ اصْطَفى لَكُمُ الدِّينَ فَلا تَمُوتُنَّ إِلاَّ وَأَنْتُمْ مُسْلِمُونَ (132)","quran_en":"130. Who turns away from Abraham’s religion except one who makes a fool of himself? We chose him in this world, and in the Hereafter he is among the righteous. 131. When his Lord said to him, “Surrender,” he said, “I have surrendered to the Lord of the worlds.” 132. Abraham enjoined this upon his sons, as did Jacob: “My sons, God has chosen the religion for you; do not die except in surrender.”","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Safiha nafsahu and the testament","html":"<p>I say: “who” is a disapproving interrogative containing a negative sense, the subject of which “turns away…” is the predicate. “Except” overturns that implicit negation. On the preferred analysis, “one who makes a fool…” is an apposition to the subject-pronoun of “turns away.” “Himself” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">نفسه<\/bdi>) is the object of <i>safiha<\/i>, which includes the meaning “does not know” or “destroys,” according to al-Zajjāj. Al-Farrāʾ takes it as specification: its pronoun retains the indefiniteness of “who” and does not make it definite. Or it follows an omitted preposition, with the verb made directly transitive, like “He struck so-and-so the back and belly.”<\/p><p>“When” is governed by “We chose him.” <i>Awṣā<\/i> and <i>waṣṣā<\/i> both mean to enjoin, though the latter suggests repetition. “This” refers to the declaration “I have surrendered” or to the religion. “Jacob” is conjoined to “Abraham,” and “My sons” is quoted after an omitted “while saying.” Alternatively, “Jacob” is a new subject whose predicate is omitted: “Jacob said, ‘My sons…’”; on that reading one pauses after “his sons.”<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “who” is a disapproving interrogative containing a negative sense, the subject of which “turns away…” is the predicate. “Except” overturns that implicit negation. On the preferred analysis, “one who makes a fool…” is an apposition to the subject-pronoun of “turns away.” “Himself” ( نفسه ) is the object of safiha , which includes the meaning “does not know” or “destroys,” according to al-Zajjāj. Al-Farrāʾ takes it as specification: its pronoun retains the indefiniteness of “who” and does not make it definite. Or it follows an omitted preposition, with the verb made directly transitive, like “He struck so-and-so the back and belly.” “When” is governed by “We chose him.” Awṣā and waṣṣā both mean to enjoin, though the latter suggests repetition. “This” refers to the declaration “I have surrendered” or to the religion. “Jacob” is conjoined to “Abraham,” and “My sons” is quoted after an omitted “while saying.” Alternatively, “Jacob” is a new subject whose predicate is omitted: “Jacob said, ‘My sons…’”; on that reading one pauses after “his sons.”"},{"type":"exposition","title":"The immediate response to the divine command","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: who would turn from Abraham’s clear religion except someone ignorant of his own worth and withholding its due, or one whose judgement is light and whose soul is foolish? How could a rational person turn from it when We chose him as a worldly leader followed by people of outward and inward knowledge, and in the Hereafter he is among those fit for Our presence and dwelling near Us?<\/p><p>We chose him because, when his Lord commanded surrender to Our judgement and obedience to Our command, he replied immediately: I surrender my face to the Lord of the worlds and yield myself wholly to Him. At death, Abraham enjoined this declaration or religion upon his four sons: Ishmael, Isaac, Midian and Midan.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-87\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B082]<\/a> His grandson Jacob likewise enjoined it upon his twelve sons, described below among the Tribes. They said: my sons, God has chosen for you the upright, clear and exalted religion. Hold to it throughout your lives; die only in surrender, adhering to it.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: who would turn from Abraham’s clear religion except someone ignorant of his own worth and withholding its due, or one whose judgement is light and whose soul is foolish? How could a rational person turn from it when We chose him as a worldly leader followed by people of outward and inward knowledge, and in the Hereafter he is among those fit for Our presence and dwelling near Us? We chose him because, when his Lord commanded surrender to Our judgement and obedience to Our command, he replied immediately: I surrender my face to the Lord of the worlds and yield myself wholly to Him. At death, Abraham enjoined this declaration or religion upon his four sons: Ishmael, Isaac, Midian and Midan. [B082] His grandson Jacob likewise enjoined it upon his twelve sons, described below among the Tribes. They said: my sons, God has chosen for you the upright, clear and exalted religion. Hold to it throughout your lives; die only in surrender, adhering to it."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Aspiration lifted above created supports","html":"<p>The religion of our father Abraham is lifting aspiration above creation, directing oneself exclusively to the True King, relinquishing intermediaries and causes, and attaching oneself to the Lord of lords. Al-Shushtarī says:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">To renounce other-than-Him is our obligation,<br>for we profess the way that effaces association and doubt.<\/blockquote><p>His religion also includes abandoning self-direction and choice, and surrendering to the decrees of the One, the All-Subduing. Whoever fully holds these qualities and enjoins them upon those he meets is made a leader in this world: his words are followed and his lights guide. In the Hereafter he is among the righteous brought near, with prophets and messengers. Whoever turns from this upright religion loses both worlds. We ask God’s protection through His favour and generosity.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"The religion of our father Abraham is lifting aspiration above creation, directing oneself exclusively to the True King, relinquishing intermediaries and causes, and attaching oneself to the Lord of lords. Al-Shushtarī says: To renounce other-than-Him is our obligation, for we profess the way that effaces association and doubt. His religion also includes abandoning self-direction and choice, and surrendering to the decrees of the One, the All-Subduing. Whoever fully holds these qualities and enjoins them upon those he meets is made a leader in this world: his words are followed and his lights guide. In the Hereafter he is among the righteous brought near, with prophets and messengers. Whoever turns from this upright religion loses both worlds. We ask God’s protection through His favour and generosity."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>When the Jews claimed that Judaism was Abraham’s religion, God declared their claim false, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"When the Jews claimed that Judaism was Abraham’s religion, God declared their claim false, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":2971,"arabic_characters":2503},{"id":"q2-133-134","q":"2:133–134","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:133–134 · The fathers’ religion and responsibility for one’s own deeds","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 168–169","cairo":"I:168–169","source_page_first":168,"source_page_last":169,"ar":"أَمْ كُنْتُمْ شُهَداءَ إِذْ حَضَرَ يَعْقُوبَ الْمَوْتُ إِذْ قالَ لِبَنِيهِ ما تَعْبُدُونَ مِنْ بَعْدِي قالُوا نَعْبُدُ إِلهَكَ وَإِلهَ آبائِكَ إِبْراهِيمَ وَإِسْماعِيلَ وَإِسْحاقَ إِلهاً واحِداً وَنَحْنُ لَهُ مُسْلِمُونَ (133) تِلْكَ أُمَّةٌ قَدْ خَلَتْ لَها ما كَسَبَتْ وَلَكُمْ ما كَسَبْتُمْ وَلا تُسْئَلُونَ عَمَّا كانُوا يَعْمَلُونَ (134)\nقلت: (أم) منقطعة، والاستفهام فيها للإنكار، أي: ما كنتم حاضرين حين حضر يعقوب الموت، وقال لبنيه ما قال، فكيف تدعون اليهودية عليه، و (إلهاً واحداً) بدل من (إله آبائك) ، وفائدته التصريح بالتوحيد، ونفى التوهم الناشئ عن تكرير المضاف، لتعذر العطف على المجرور، والتأكيد، أو نصب على الاختصاص أو الحال، وعد إسماعيل من الآباء تغليباً، أو لأنه كالأب لقوله- عليه الصلاة والسلام-: «عَمُّ الرُجلِ صِنْوُ أبيه» وقال في العباس: «هذا بقية آبائي» . قاله البيضاوي.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله في توبيخ اليهود على زعمهم أن اليهودية كانت ملة إبراهيم، وأن يعقوب عليه السلام أوصى بها عند موته، فقال: هل كنتم حاضرين عند يعقوب حين حضرته الوفاة حتى أوصى بما زعمتم؟ وإنما كانت وصيته أن قال لبنيه: ما تَعْبُدُونَ مِنْ بَعْدِي أيْ: أيّ شيء تعبدونه؟ أراد به تقريرهم على التوحيد وأخذ ميثاقهم على الثبات عليه، (قالوا) في جوابه: نَعْبُدُ إِلهَكَ المتفق على وجوب وجوده وثبوت ألوهيته الذي هو إِلهَكَ وَإِلهَ آبائِكَ من قبلك إِبْراهِيمَ وولده إِسْماعِيلَ وَإِسْحاقَ الذي هو إلهٌ واحد. ونحن منقادون لأحكامه، مستسلمون لأمره إلى مماتنا، فلم يوص يعقوب إلا بما سمعتم، فانتسابكم يا معشر اليهود إليهم لا يوجب انتفاعكم بأعمالهم، وإنما تنتفعون بموافقتهم واتباعهم.\nفتلك أُمَّةٌ أي: جماعة قَدْ خَلَتْ لَها ما كَسَبَتْ من الخير، وَلَكُمْ ما كَسَبْتُمْ أنتم، وَلا تُسْئَلُونَ عَمَّا كانُوا يَعْمَلُونَ فلا تؤاخذون بسيئاتهم، كما لا تثابون بحسناتهم. وهذا كما قال صلّى الله عليه وسلّم لقريش: «لا يأتيني الناسُ بأعمَالهِم وتَأتُوني بأنْسَابِكُم» .\nالإشارة: يقال لمن حصرَ الخصوصية في أسلافه، ونفاها عن غيرهم: هل حضرتم معهم حين أوصوا بذلك؟ بل ما كانوا يوصون إلا بإخلاص العبودية، وتوحيد الألوهية، ومشاهدة عظمة الربوبية، فمن حصّل هذه الخصال كانت الخصوصية معه أينما كان، ومَن حاد عنها ومال إلى متابعة الهوى انتقلت إلى غيره، ويقال له: إن أسلافه قد جَدُّوا ووجَدُوا، وأنت لا تنتفع بأعمالهم في طريق الخصوصية، (تلك أمة قد خلت لها ما كسبت ولكم ما كسبتم ... ) الآية. وبالله التوفيق.\nولما أمر اليهود والنصارى المسلمين باتباع دينهم، لأنه أقدم، ردّ الله عليهم، فقال:","quran_ar":"أَمْ كُنْتُمْ شُهَداءَ إِذْ حَضَرَ يَعْقُوبَ الْمَوْتُ إِذْ قالَ لِبَنِيهِ ما تَعْبُدُونَ مِنْ بَعْدِي قالُوا نَعْبُدُ إِلهَكَ وَإِلهَ آبائِكَ إِبْراهِيمَ وَإِسْماعِيلَ وَإِسْحاقَ إِلهاً واحِداً وَنَحْنُ لَهُ مُسْلِمُونَ (133) تِلْكَ أُمَّةٌ قَدْ خَلَتْ لَها ما كَسَبَتْ وَلَكُمْ ما كَسَبْتُمْ وَلا تُسْئَلُونَ عَمَّا كانُوا يَعْمَلُونَ (134)","quran_en":"133. Were you present when death came to Jacob, when he asked his sons, “What will you worship after me?” They replied, “We shall worship your God and the God of your fathers, Abraham, Ishmael and Isaac: one God. To Him we surrender.” 134. That was a community which has passed away. Theirs is what they earned, and yours what you have earned. You will not be questioned about what they used to do.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"One God, not several","html":"<p>I say: <i>am<\/i> is disconnected (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">منقطعة<\/bdi>), its interrogative expressing denial: you were not present at Jacob’s death when he spoke to his sons, so how can you attribute Judaism to him? “One God” is an apposition to “the God of your fathers.” It explicitly affirms unity, removes any misconception caused by repeating the annexed noun—which is necessary because one cannot here conjoin directly to the genitive pronoun—and reinforces the sense. Alternatively, it is accusative through specification or as a circumstantial expression.<\/p><p>Ishmael is counted among the fathers by giving the predominant class’s designation to the whole (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التغليب<\/bdi>), or because he is like a father: the Prophet said, “A man’s paternal uncle is the counterpart of his father,” and of al-ʿAbbās, “This is what remains of my fathers.” Thus al-Bayḍāwī.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: am is disconnected ( منقطعة ), its interrogative expressing denial: you were not present at Jacob’s death when he spoke to his sons, so how can you attribute Judaism to him? “One God” is an apposition to “the God of your fathers.” It explicitly affirms unity, removes any misconception caused by repeating the annexed noun—which is necessary because one cannot here conjoin directly to the genitive pronoun—and reinforces the sense. Alternatively, it is accusative through specification or as a circumstantial expression. Ishmael is counted among the fathers by giving the predominant class’s designation to the whole ( التغليب ), or because he is like a father: the Prophet said, “A man’s paternal uncle is the counterpart of his father,” and of al-ʿAbbās, “This is what remains of my fathers.” Thus al-Bayḍāwī."},{"type":"exposition","title":"A covenant of unity, not an inherited claim","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, reproaches the Jews for claiming that Judaism was Abraham’s religion and Jacob’s dying testament. Were you there to hear the testament you allege? He actually asked his sons what they would worship after him, confirming them in unity and taking their covenant of steadfastness. They answered: we worship your God, whose necessary existence and divinity are acknowledged, the God of your fathers Abraham and his sons Ishmael and Isaac—one God. We yield to His judgements and surrender to His command until death.<\/p><p>Jacob enjoined only what you have heard. Your descent from them does not let you profit from their deeds; only conforming to and following them does.<\/p><p>That community has passed: it has the good it earned, and you have what you earned. You are not questioned about their acts: neither punished for their wrongs nor rewarded for their good. This resembles the Prophet’s words to Quraysh: “Do not let people come to me with their deeds while you come with your lineages.”<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, reproaches the Jews for claiming that Judaism was Abraham’s religion and Jacob’s dying testament. Were you there to hear the testament you allege? He actually asked his sons what they would worship after him, confirming them in unity and taking their covenant of steadfastness. They answered: we worship your God, whose necessary existence and divinity are acknowledged, the God of your fathers Abraham and his sons Ishmael and Isaac—one God. We yield to His judgements and surrender to His command until death. Jacob enjoined only what you have heard. Your descent from them does not let you profit from their deeds; only conforming to and following them does. That community has passed: it has the good it earned, and you have what you earned. You are not questioned about their acts: neither punished for their wrongs nor rewarded for their good. This resembles the Prophet’s words to Quraysh: “Do not let people come to me with their deeds while you come with your lineages.”"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Ancestry does not replace realization","html":"<p>Someone restricting special election to his forefathers and denying it elsewhere is asked: were you present when they enjoined such a claim? They enjoined only sincere servanthood, the unity of Divinity and witnessing Lordship’s grandeur. Whoever realizes these qualities possesses special election wherever he is. If he deviates and follows desire, it passes to another. He is told: your forefathers strove and found; their deeds do not benefit you in the path of special election. “That community has passed; theirs is what they earned, yours what you earned.” Through God is success.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Someone restricting special election to his forefathers and denying it elsewhere is asked: were you present when they enjoined such a claim? They enjoined only sincere servanthood, the unity of Divinity and witnessing Lordship’s grandeur. Whoever realizes these qualities possesses special election wherever he is. If he deviates and follows desire, it passes to another. He is told: your forefathers strove and found; their deeds do not benefit you in the path of special election. “That community has passed; theirs is what they earned, yours what you earned.” Through God is success."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>When Jews and Christians told the Muslims to follow their older religion, God replied:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"When Jews and Christians told the Muslims to follow their older religion, God replied:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":2505,"arabic_characters":2233},{"id":"q2-135","q":"2:135","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:135 · Following the guide who points to God","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 169–170","cairo":"I:169–170","source_page_first":169,"source_page_last":170,"ar":"وَقالُوا كُونُوا هُوداً أَوْ نَصارى تَهْتَدُوا قُلْ بَلْ مِلَّةَ إِبْراهِيمَ حَنِيفاً وَما كانَ مِنَ الْمُشْرِكِينَ (135)\nقلت: الضمير في (قالوا) لأهل الكتاب، و (أو) للتفصيل، أي: قالت اليهود: كونوا هودا، وقالت النصارى:\nكونوا نصارى. و (تهتدوا) جواب الأمر، و (ملة) منصوب بفعل محذوف، على حذف مضاف، أي: بل نكون أهل ملة إبراهيم، أو نتبع او نلزم ملة إبراهيم، و (حنيفاً) حال من المضاف إليه، لأنه كجزئه، أي: مائلاً عن الباطل إلى الحق.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وقالت اليهود للمسلمين: كُونُوا معنا هوداً تَهْتَدُوا فإن ديننا أقدم، وقالت النصارى لهم أيضاً: كونوا نَصارى معنا تَهْتَدُوا فإن ديننا أصوب، قُلْ لهم يا محمد: بَلْ نلزم مِلَّةَ إِبْراهِيمَ الذي كان مائلاً عن الباطل متبعاً للحق، ومشاهداً له وحده. ولم يكن من المشركين كما أشركتم بعُزير وعيسى وغيرهما، تعالى الله عن قولكم علواً كبيراً.\nالإشارة: قد سرى هذا الطبع في بعض المنتسبين، يُرَغِّبُون الناس في طريقهم، ويحرصون على اتباعهم والدخول معهم، وينقصون طريق غيرهم، وهو وصف مذموم، بل الواجب أن ينظر الإنسان بعين البصيرة، فمن وجده يدل على الله ويغيب عما سواه، ينهض حاله ويدل على الله مقاله، اتبعه وحطَّ رأسه له، ولزم ملته وطريقه أينما كان، وكيفما كان. ومن وجده على غير هذا الوصف، أعرض عنه، والتمس غيره، وليس من شأن الدعاة إلى\nالله الحرص على الناس، أو الترغيب في اتباعهم، بل هم أزهد الناس في الناس، من أتاهم دلّوه على الله، ومن لقيهم نصحوه في الله، هم على قدَم الرسول صلّى الله عليه وسلّم وقد قال له الحقّ تعالى: أَفَأَنْتَ تُكْرِهُ النَّاسَ حَتَّى يَكُونُوا مُؤْمِنِينَ. لَسْتَ عَلَيْهِمْ بِمُصَيْطِرٍ.، لَعَلَّكَ باخِعٌ نَفْسَكَ أَلَّا يَكُونُوا مُؤْمِنِينَ، فكان صلّى الله عليه وسلّم بعد ذلك يدل على الله وينظر ما يفعل الله. وبالله التوفيق.\nثم بيَّن الحق تعالى كيفية الإيمان الذي يجب اتباعه، وأبطل ما سواه، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَقالُوا كُونُوا هُوداً أَوْ نَصارى تَهْتَدُوا قُلْ بَلْ مِلَّةَ إِبْراهِيمَ حَنِيفاً وَما كانَ مِنَ الْمُشْرِكِينَ (135)","quran_en":"135. They say, “Be Jews or Christians and you will be guided.” Say, “Rather, Abraham’s religion, inclining wholly to the truth; he was not among those who associate partners with God.”","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The two claims and the upright inclination","html":"<p>I say: “they said” refers to the People of the Book; “or” distributes the statement: the Jews say “be Jews,” the Christians “be Christians.” “You will be guided” is the response to the command. “Religion” is accusative through an omitted verb and, on one analysis, an omitted annexed noun: rather, we become adherents of Abraham’s religion; or, we follow or adhere to his religion. “Inclining wholly” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حنيفاً<\/bdi>) is circumstantial with respect to the second term of the annexation, since the first is like part of it: inclining from falsehood towards truth.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “they said” refers to the People of the Book; “or” distributes the statement: the Jews say “be Jews,” the Christians “be Christians.” “You will be guided” is the response to the command. “Religion” is accusative through an omitted verb and, on one analysis, an omitted annexed noun: rather, we become adherents of Abraham’s religion; or, we follow or adhere to his religion. “Inclining wholly” ( حنيفاً ) is circumstantial with respect to the second term of the annexation, since the first is like part of it: inclining from falsehood towards truth."},{"type":"exposition","title":"The religion free of association","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: the Jews tell Muslims to join them and be guided because their religion is older; the Christians tell them to join theirs because it is more correct. Say, Muḥammad: rather, we adhere to Abraham’s religion. He inclined away from falsehood, followed truth and witnessed Him alone. He was not an associator, as you associated ʿUzayr, Jesus and others. Exalted is God far above your assertions.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: the Jews tell Muslims to join them and be guided because their religion is older; the Christians tell them to join theirs because it is more correct. Say, Muḥammad: rather, we adhere to Abraham’s religion. He inclined away from falsehood, followed truth and witnessed Him alone. He was not an associator, as you associated ʿUzayr, Jesus and others. Exalted is God far above your assertions."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Spiritual calling without recruitment and rivalry","html":"<p>This disposition has spread among some affiliated to spiritual paths. They entice people to their own way, eagerly seek followers and belittle other paths: a blameworthy trait. Instead, one must look with insight. Whoever points to God and makes one absent from other-than-Him, whose state raises one and whose speech directs one to God—follow him, lower your head before him and adhere to his way, wherever and however he may be. Turn from anyone lacking this quality and seek another.<\/p><p>Those who call to God are not characterized by grasping after people<\/p><p>or enticing them to become followers. They are the least attached to people. Whoever comes, they direct to God; whoever meets them, they counsel for God. They stand upon the Messenger’s footing. The Real told him, “Will you compel people until they become believers?” [10:99]; “You are not a controller over them” [88:22]; and “Perhaps you would destroy yourself because they do not believe” [26:3]. Thereafter he pointed to God and watched what God would do. Through God is success.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"This disposition has spread among some affiliated to spiritual paths. They entice people to their own way, eagerly seek followers and belittle other paths: a blameworthy trait. Instead, one must look with insight. Whoever points to God and makes one absent from other-than-Him, whose state raises one and whose speech directs one to God—follow him, lower your head before him and adhere to his way, wherever and however he may be. Turn from anyone lacking this quality and seek another. Those who call to God are not characterized by grasping after people or enticing them to become followers. They are the least attached to people. Whoever comes, they direct to God; whoever meets them, they counsel for God. They stand upon the Messenger’s footing. The Real told him, “Will you compel people until they become believers?” [10:99]; “You are not a controller over them” [88:22]; and “Perhaps you would destroy yourself because they do not believe” [26:3]. Thereafter he pointed to God and watched what God would do. Through God is success."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real explains the faith that must be followed and invalidates what differs from it:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real explains the faith that must be followed and invalidates what differs from it:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":2113,"arabic_characters":1652},{"id":"q2-136-138","q":"2:136–138","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:136–138 · Faith in all the messengers and the divine dye","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 170–172","cairo":"I:170–172","source_page_first":170,"source_page_last":172,"ar":"قُولُوا آمَنَّا بِاللَّهِ وَما أُنْزِلَ إِلَيْنا وَما أُنْزِلَ إِلى إِبْراهِيمَ وَإِسْماعِيلَ وَإِسْحاقَ وَيَعْقُوبَ وَالْأَسْباطِ وَما أُوتِيَ مُوسى وَعِيسى وَما أُوتِيَ النَّبِيُّونَ مِنْ رَبِّهِمْ لاَ نُفَرِّقُ بَيْنَ أَحَدٍ مِنْهُمْ وَنَحْنُ لَهُ مُسْلِمُونَ (136) فَإِنْ آمَنُوا بِمِثْلِ ما آمَنْتُمْ بِهِ فَقَدِ اهْتَدَوْا وَإِنْ تَوَلَّوْا فَإِنَّما هُمْ فِي شِقاقٍ فَسَيَكْفِيكَهُمُ اللَّهُ وَهُوَ السَّمِيعُ الْعَلِيمُ (137) صِبْغَةَ اللَّهِ وَمَنْ أَحْسَنُ مِنَ اللَّهِ صِبْغَةً وَنَحْنُ لَهُ عابِدُونَ (138)\nقلت: الأسباط: الأحفاد، والسِّبطُ في بني إسرائيل بمنزلة القبيلة في ولد إسماعيل، والباء في (بمثل) : يحتمل أن تكون زائدة كقوله تعالى: وجَزاءُ سَيِّئَةٍ بِمِثْلِها، أو (مثل) مُقْحَم، أي: فإن آمنوا بما آمنتم به، كقوله تعالى:\nوَشَهِدَ شاهِدٌ مِنْ بَنِي إِسْرائِيلَ عَلى مِثْلِهِ. والشقاق: المخالفة، كأنَّ كل واحد من المتخالفين في شق غير شق الآخر، و (صبغة الله) : مصدر مؤكد لآمنا لأن الإيمان ينصبغ في القلوب، ويظهر أثره على الجوارح ظهور الصبغ على المصبوغ، ويتداخل في قلوبهم تداخل الصبغ للثوب. أي: آمنا وصبغنا الله به صبغة.\nوهي فطرةَ الله التي فطر الناس عليها، وعبَّر عنها بالصبغ للمشاكلة فإن النصارى كانوا يغمسون أولادهم في ماء أصفر يسمونه المعمودية، ويقولون: هو تطهير لهم، وبه تحق نصرانيتهم، فردَّ الله تعالى عليهم بأن صبغة، الله أحسن من صبغتهم وقيل: نصب على البدل من (ملة إبراهيم) ، أو على الإغراء، أي: الزموا صبغة الله.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: قُولُوا يا معشر المسلمين في تحقيق إيمانكم: آمَنَّا بِاللَّهِ أي: صدقنا بوجوده متصفاً بصفة الكمال، منزّهاً عن النقائص، وَبما أُنْزِلَ إِلَيْنا وهو القرآن، وَبما أُنْزِلَ من الصحف إِلى إِبْراهِيمَ وَإِسْماعِيلَ وَإِسْحاقَ وَيَعْقُوبَ ولد إسحاق، وَالْأَسْباطِ أولاد يعقوب عليه السلام وهم: روبيل وشمعون ولاوى ويهوذا وربالون ويشحُرْ، ودنية بنته، وأمهم لَيَا، ثم خلف على أختها راحيل، فولدت له يوسف وبنيامين، وَوُلد له من سرِّيَّتينِ: دان ونفتالى وجاد وآشر.\nقال ابن حجر: اختلف في نبوتهم، فقيل: كانوا أنبياء، وقيل: لم يكن فيهم نبيّ، وإنما المراد بالأسباط قبائل من بني إسرائيل، فقد كان فيهم من الأنبياء عدد كثير. هـ. وممن صرّح بنفي نبوتهم عياض وجمهور المفسرين.\nانظر: المحشي الفاسي.\nوقولوا: آمنا بما أنزل إلى مُوسى وهو التوراة، وَعِيسى وهو الإنجيل، وبما أُوتِيَ النَّبِيُّونَ كلهم مِنْ رَبِّهِمْ من عرفنا منهم ومن لم نعرف، لاَ نُفَرِّقُ بَيْنَ أَحَدٍ واحد مِنْهُمْ كما فرقت اليهود والنصارى، فقد آمنا بالله وبجميع أنبيائه وَنَحْنُ لَهُ مُسْلِمُونَ أي: منقادون لأحكامه الظاهرة والباطنة.\nقال الحق جلّ جلاله: فَإِنْ آمَنُوا أي: أهل الكتاب إيماناً مثل إيمانكم، فَقَدِ اهْتَدَوْا إلى الحق والصواب، وإن أعرضوا عن ذلك فاتركهم حتى نأمرك فيهم، فَإِنَّما هُمْ فِي شِقاقٍ وخلاف لك، فلا تهتم بشأنهم، فَسَيَكْفِيكَهُمُ اللَّهُ أي سيكفيك شرهم وينصرك عليهم، وَهُوَ السَّمِيعُ لدعائكم، الْعَلِيمُ بإخلاصكم، فالزموا صِبْغَةَ اللَّهِ التي صُبغتم بها، وهى الإيمان بما ذكرت لكم فإنه لا أحسن صبغة من صبغة الله، وَقولوا: نَحْنُ لَهُ عابِدُونَ.\nالإشارة: كما أوجب الله تعالى الإيمان بجميع الرسل في طريق العموم، كذلك أوجب الله التصديق بكل من ثبتت ولايته في طريق الخصوص، فمن فرق بينهم فقد كفر بطريقهم، ومن كفر بطريقهم طُرد عن بابهم، ومن طرد عن بابهم طرد عن باب الله، لأن إسقاطه من الولاية إيذاء له ، ومن آذى وليًا فقد آذن الله بالحرب، فالواجب، على مَن أراد أن يرد مناهلهم، أن يصدق بجميعهم، ويعظم من انتسب إليهم، حتى تنصبغ في قلبه حلاوة الإيمان، وتشرق عليه شموس العرفان، فمن فعل هذا فقد اهتدى إلى الحق والصواب، واستحق الدخول مع الأحباب، ومن أعرض عن هذا فإنما هو في شقاق، وربما يخاف عليه من شؤم الكفر والنفاق، فسيكفي الله أولياءه سوءَ شره، والله غالب على أمره.\nقال القشيري: فللقلوب صبغة، وللأرواح صبغة، وللسرائر صبغة، وللظواهر صبغة، فصبغة الأشباح والظواهر بآثار التوفيق، وصبغة الأرواح والسرائر بأنوار التحقيق. هـ. وقال الورتجبي: صبغة الله: صفته الخاصة\nالتي خلق آدم عليها، وأورثت ذلك في أرواح ذريته من الأنبياء والأولياء. ثم قال: وسقاها من شراب الزلفة، وألهمها خصائص علوم الربوبية، فاستنارت بنور المعرفة، وخاضت في بحر الربوبية، وخرجت منها تجليات أسرار الوحدانية، وتكوّنت بصبغ الصفات. هـ. وبالله التوفيق.\nولمَّا ادعت اليهود والنصارى أنهم أولى الناس بالله من غيرهم لتقدم دينهم، رد الله عليهم ووبخهم فقال: (قل أتحاجوننا ... ) الآية. وقيل: إن اليهود قالوا للنبى صلّى الله عليه وسلّم: الأنبياء كلهم منا، فلو كنت نبيا لكنت منا، فردَّ الله عليهم بقوله:","quran_ar":"قُولُوا آمَنَّا بِاللَّهِ وَما أُنْزِلَ إِلَيْنا وَما أُنْزِلَ إِلى إِبْراهِيمَ وَإِسْماعِيلَ وَإِسْحاقَ وَيَعْقُوبَ وَالْأَسْباطِ وَما أُوتِيَ مُوسى وَعِيسى وَما أُوتِيَ النَّبِيُّونَ مِنْ رَبِّهِمْ لاَ نُفَرِّقُ بَيْنَ أَحَدٍ مِنْهُمْ وَنَحْنُ لَهُ مُسْلِمُونَ (136) فَإِنْ آمَنُوا بِمِثْلِ ما آمَنْتُمْ بِهِ فَقَدِ اهْتَدَوْا وَإِنْ تَوَلَّوْا فَإِنَّما هُمْ فِي شِقاقٍ فَسَيَكْفِيكَهُمُ اللَّهُ وَهُوَ السَّمِيعُ الْعَلِيمُ (137) صِبْغَةَ اللَّهِ وَمَنْ أَحْسَنُ مِنَ اللَّهِ صِبْغَةً وَنَحْنُ لَهُ عابِدُونَ (138)","quran_en":"136. Say, “We believe in God, in what has been sent down to us, and what was sent down to Abraham, Ishmael, Isaac, Jacob and the Tribes; in what Moses and Jesus were given, and what all the prophets were given by their Lord. We make no distinction between any of them; to Him we surrender.” 137. If they believe as you believe, they are guided. If they turn away, they are only in opposition; God will suffice you against them. He is the All-Hearing, the All-Knowing. 138. God’s dye—and whose dye is better than God’s? We worship Him alone.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Tribes, opposition and the dye of faith","html":"<p>I say: the <i>asbāṭ<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أسباط<\/bdi>) are descendants; a <i>sibṭ<\/i> among the Children of Israel is like a tribe among Ishmael’s descendants. The bāʾ in <i>bi-mithli<\/i> may be supplementary, as in “the recompense of an evil deed is its like” [10:27]; or <i>mithl<\/i> itself is inserted for reinforcement: if they believe what you believe, as in “a witness from the Children of Israel testified to its like” [46:10]. Opposition (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">شقاق<\/bdi>) is disagreement, as though each opponent occupies a different side (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">شقّ<\/bdi>).<\/p><p>“God’s dye” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">صبغة الله<\/bdi>) is a verbal noun reinforcing “we believe,” for faith dyes hearts and shows its effect upon the limbs as dye upon a dyed object, penetrating hearts as dye penetrates cloth: we believe, and God dyes us with it. It is the original disposition upon which God created people. “Dye” is used through verbal correspondence (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المشاكلة<\/bdi>), since Christians immersed children in yellow water called baptism, regarding it as purification establishing their Christianity. God replies that His dye is better.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-88\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B083]<\/a> Alternatively, it is an apposition to “Abraham’s religion,” or an accusative of exhortation: hold to God’s dye.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: the asbāṭ ( أسباط ) are descendants; a sibṭ among the Children of Israel is like a tribe among Ishmael’s descendants. The bāʾ in bi-mithli may be supplementary, as in “the recompense of an evil deed is its like” [10:27]; or mithl itself is inserted for reinforcement: if they believe what you believe, as in “a witness from the Children of Israel testified to its like” [46:10]. Opposition ( شقاق ) is disagreement, as though each opponent occupies a different side ( شقّ ). “God’s dye” ( صبغة الله ) is a verbal noun reinforcing “we believe,” for faith dyes hearts and shows its effect upon the limbs as dye upon a dyed object, penetrating hearts as dye penetrates cloth: we believe, and God dyes us with it. It is the original disposition upon which God created people. “Dye” is used through verbal correspondence ( المشاكلة ), since Christians immersed children in yellow water called baptism, regarding it as purification establishing their Christianity. God replies that His dye is better. [B083] Alternatively, it is an apposition to “Abraham’s religion,” or an accusative of exhortation: hold to God’s dye."},{"type":"exposition","title":"The prophets and the descendants of Jacob","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: Muslims, declare in realization of your faith: we believe in God—His existence, perfection and transcendence beyond defects; in the Qurʾān revealed to us; and in the scrolls revealed to Abraham, Ishmael, Isaac, Jacob son of Isaac, and the Tribes, Jacob’s sons. These are Reuben, Simeon, Levi, Judah, Zebulun and Issachar, with their sister Dinah, whose mother was Leah. He subsequently married Leah’s sister Rachel, who bore Joseph and Benjamin. Two concubines bore Dan, Naphtali, Gad and Asher.<\/p><p>Ibn Ḥajar says their prophethood is disputed: some hold that they were prophets; others that none was a prophet, and that “the Tribes” means the tribes of Israel, among whom many prophets arose. ʿIyāḍ and the majority of commentators expressly deny their prophethood: consult the glossator al-Fāsī.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-89\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B084]<\/a><\/p><p>Say: we believe what was given to Moses—the Torah—and Jesus—the Gospel—and all prophets by their Lord, those we know and those we do not. We distinguish none by rejection, as Jews and Christians did. We believe God and all His prophets and surrender to His outward and inward judgements.<\/p><p>The Real says: if the People of the Book believe with faith like yours, they have reached truth and correctness. If they turn away, leave them until We command you concerning them. They are in opposition and disagreement; do not be troubled by them. God will suffice against their evil and grant you victory. He hears your prayer and knows your sincerity. Hold to the divine dye with which you were coloured—faith in all I have mentioned—for none is better. Say: we worship Him alone.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: Muslims, declare in realization of your faith: we believe in God—His existence, perfection and transcendence beyond defects; in the Qurʾān revealed to us; and in the scrolls revealed to Abraham, Ishmael, Isaac, Jacob son of Isaac, and the Tribes, Jacob’s sons. These are Reuben, Simeon, Levi, Judah, Zebulun and Issachar, with their sister Dinah, whose mother was Leah. He subsequently married Leah’s sister Rachel, who bore Joseph and Benjamin. Two concubines bore Dan, Naphtali, Gad and Asher. Ibn Ḥajar says their prophethood is disputed: some hold that they were prophets; others that none was a prophet, and that “the Tribes” means the tribes of Israel, among whom many prophets arose. ʿIyāḍ and the majority of commentators expressly deny their prophethood: consult the glossator al-Fāsī. [B084] Say: we believe what was given to Moses—the Torah—and Jesus—the Gospel—and all prophets by their Lord, those we know and those we do not. We distinguish none by rejection, as Jews and Christians did. We believe God and all His prophets and surrender to His outward and inward judgements. The Real says: if the People of the Book believe with faith like yours, they have reached truth and correctness. If they turn away, leave them until We command you concerning them. They are in opposition and disagreement; do not be troubled by them. God will suffice against their evil and grant you victory. He hears your prayer and knows your sincerity. Hold to the divine dye with which you were coloured—faith in all I have mentioned—for none is better. Say: we worship Him alone."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Affirming every established friend of God","html":"<p>Just as God requires faith in all messengers on the general path, so He requires affirmation of everyone whose friendship with Him is established on the special path. Whoever divides between them denies their way; whoever denies their way is expelled from their door, and whoever is expelled from their door is expelled from God’s. To strip a friend of his friendship is to hurt him; whoever hurts God’s friend declares war against God. Anyone wishing to drink at their watering places must affirm them all and honour those affiliated to them, until faith’s sweetness dyes his heart and the suns of gnosis shine upon him.<\/p><p>Whoever does this reaches truth and deserves admission among the beloved. Whoever turns away is in opposition; one may fear for him the ill omen of unbelief and hypocrisy. God will suffice His friends against his evil, and God prevails in His purpose.<\/p><p>Al-Qushayrī says: hearts have a dye, spirits a dye, secrets a dye and outward forms a dye. Bodies and outward forms are dyed with the effects of divine enabling; spirits and secrets with the lights of realization. Al-Wartajbī says: “God’s dye is His special attribute upon which He created Adam,<\/p><p>inherited in the spirits of Adam’s prophetic and saintly descendants.” He then said: “He gave them the wine of nearness and inspired them with the special sciences of Lordship. They shone with gnosis’s light, plunged into Lordship’s sea, and from them came disclosures of the mysteries of unity; they were formed through the dye of the Attributes.” Through God is success.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Just as God requires faith in all messengers on the general path, so He requires affirmation of everyone whose friendship with Him is established on the special path. Whoever divides between them denies their way; whoever denies their way is expelled from their door, and whoever is expelled from their door is expelled from God’s. To strip a friend of his friendship is to hurt him; whoever hurts God’s friend declares war against God. Anyone wishing to drink at their watering places must affirm them all and honour those affiliated to them, until faith’s sweetness dyes his heart and the suns of gnosis shine upon him. Whoever does this reaches truth and deserves admission among the beloved. Whoever turns away is in opposition; one may fear for him the ill omen of unbelief and hypocrisy. God will suffice His friends against his evil, and God prevails in His purpose. Al-Qushayrī says: hearts have a dye, spirits a dye, secrets a dye and outward forms a dye. Bodies and outward forms are dyed with the effects of divine enabling; spirits and secrets with the lights of realization. Al-Wartajbī says: “God’s dye is His special attribute upon which He created Adam, inherited in the spirits of Adam’s prophetic and saintly descendants.” He then said: “He gave them the wine of nearness and inspired them with the special sciences of Lordship. They shone with gnosis’s light, plunged into Lordship’s sea, and from them came disclosures of the mysteries of unity; they were formed through the dye of the Attributes.” Through God is success."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>When Jews and Christians claimed greater proximity to God than others because their religion was older, He rebuked them: “Say, do you dispute with us…”. Another account says the Jews told the Prophet, “All prophets are from us; were you a prophet, you would be from us.” God replied:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"When Jews and Christians claimed greater proximity to God than others because their religion was older, He rebuked them: “Say, do you dispute with us…”. Another account says the Jews told the Prophet, “All prophets are from us; were you a prophet, you would be from us.” God replied:"}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C171.1","page":171,"ar":"الولي لا ينظر إلى الخلق بل غايته - رضا الله عنه - فإنكار الناس ولاية ولي لا يؤذي الولي، وإنما أذى الإنكار يعود على المنكر نفسه، طبقا للحديث الوارد.","en":"The saint does not look to created beings; his goal is God’s approval of him. People’s denial of a saint’s sainthood therefore does not harm the saint: the harm of denial returns to the denier himself, in accordance with the report cited.","anchor":"لأن إسقاطه من الولاية إيذاء له"}],"english_characters":4560,"arabic_characters":4061},{"id":"q2-139-141","q":"2:139–141","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:139–141 · Sincerity, not occupation or ancestry, distinguishes people","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 172–173","cairo":"I:172–173","source_page_first":172,"source_page_last":173,"ar":"قُلْ أَتُحَاجُّونَنا فِي اللَّهِ وَهُوَ رَبُّنا وَرَبُّكُمْ وَلَنا أَعْمالُنا وَلَكُمْ أَعْمالُكُمْ وَنَحْنُ لَهُ مُخْلِصُونَ (139) أَمْ تَقُولُونَ إِنَّ إِبْراهِيمَ وَإِسْماعِيلَ وَإِسْحاقَ وَيَعْقُوبَ وَالْأَسْباطَ كانُوا هُوداً أَوْ نَصارى قُلْ أَأَنْتُمْ أَعْلَمُ أَمِ اللَّهُ وَمَنْ أَظْلَمُ مِمَّنْ كَتَمَ شَهادَةً عِنْدَهُ مِنَ اللَّهِ وَمَا اللَّهُ بِغافِلٍ عَمَّا تَعْمَلُونَ (140) تِلْكَ أُمَّةٌ قَدْ خَلَتْ لَها ما كَسَبَتْ وَلَكُمْ ما كَسَبْتُمْ وَلا تُسْئَلُونَ عَمَّا كانُوا يَعْمَلُونَ (141)\nقلت: الذي يظهر أن (أم) منقطعة، بمعنى بل، على قراءة الخطاب والغيبة لأن المقصود إنكار وقوع الأمرين معاً، لا أحدهما.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: قُلْ يا محمد لأهل الكتاب: أتخاصموننا فِي اللَّهِ وتقولون: أنتم أولى به منا وَهُوَ رَبُّنا وَرَبُّكُمْ، لا يختص به واحد دون آخر، وَلَنا أَعْمالُنا نتقرب بها إليه، وَلَكُمْ أَعْمالُكُمْ تتقربون بها أيضاً، فكيف تختصون به دوننا وَنَحْنُ لَهُ مُخْلِصُونَ في أعمالنا وقلوبنا دونكم فإنكم أشركتم به غيره، فإن قلتم: إن الأنبياء كلهم منكم وعلى ملتكم فقد كذبتم، أتقولون إِنَّ إِبْراهِيمَ وَإِسْماعِيلَ وَإِسْحاقَ وَيَعْقُوبَ وأولاده الْأَسْباطَ كانُوا هُوداً على دينكم يا معشر اليهود، أَوْ نَصارى على ملتكم يا معشر النصارى.\nقُلْ لهم يا محمد: أَأَنْتُمْ أَعْلَمُ أَمِ اللَّهُ وقد نفى الأمرين معاً عن إبراهيم فقال: مَا كانَ إِبْراهِيمُ يَهُودِيًّا وَلا نَصْرانِيًّا وَلكِنْ كانَ حَنِيفاً مُسْلِماً وقال: وَما أُنْزِلَتِ التَّوْراةُ وَالْإِنْجِيلُ إِلَّا مِنْ بَعْدِهِ، وهؤلاء المعطوفون عليه: أتباعه في الدين، فليسوا يهوداً ولا نصارى، فكيف تدعون أنهم كلهم منكم، وعلى دينكم، وأنتم تشهدون أنهم لم يكونوا على دينكم؟ وَمَنْ أَظْلَمُ مِمَّنْ كَتَمَ شَهادَةً عِنْدَهُ مِنَ اللَّهِ، وهي شهادة الحق\nلإبراهيم بالحنيفية، والبراءة من اليهودية والنصرانية، أي: لا أحد أظلم منه، وليس الله تعالى بِغافِلٍ عَمَّا تَعْمَلُونَ، بل يجازيكم على النقير والقطمير، فإن اعتمدتم على نسبكم إليهم فقد اغتررتم.\nتِلْكَ أُمَّةٌ قد مضت، لَها ما كَسَبَتْ لا ينتفع به غيرها، وَلَكُمْ ما كَسَبْتُمْ لا ينفعكم غيره، ولا تسألون عن عملهم كما لا يسألون عن أعمالكم. قال البيضاوي: كرره للمبالغة في التحذير، والزجر عما استحكم في الطباع من الافتخار بالآباء، والاتكال عليهم، وقيل: الخطاب فيما سبق لهم، وفي هذه الآية لنا، تحذيراً عن الاقتداء بهم، وقيل: المراد بالأمة في الأولى الأنبياء، وفي الثانية أسلاف اليهود والنصارى. هـ.\nالإشارة: كل من أقامه الحقّ في وجهة، ووجهه إليها، فهو عامل لله فيها، قائم بمراد الله منها، وما اختلفت الأعمال إلا من جهة المقاصد، وما تفاوت الناس إلا من جهة الإخلاص. فالخلق كلهم عبيد للملك المجيد، وما وقع الاختصاص إلا من جهة الإخلاص. فمن كان أكثر إخلاصاً لله كان أولى من غيره بالله، وبقدر ما يقع للعبد من الصفاء يكون له من الاصطفاء، فالصوفية والعلماء والعباد والزهاد وأهل الأسباب على اختلاف أنواعهم كلهم عاملون لله، ليس أحد منهم بأولى من غيره بالله إلا من جهة الإخلاص وإفراد القلب لله، فمن ادعى الاختصاص بالله من غير هذه الوجهة فهو كاذب، ومن اعتمد على عمل غيره فهو مغرور، يقال له: تِلْكَ أُمَّةٌ قَدْ خَلَتْ لَها ما كَسَبَتْ وَلَكُمْ ما كَسَبْتُمْ وَلا تُسْئَلُونَ عَمَّا كانُوا يَعْمَلُونَ.\nولمّا أراد الله تعالى أن ينسخ القبلة من جهة الشام ويردها إلى الكعبة، أخبر أنه سينكرها قومٌ خَفَّتْ أحلامُهم، وفسدت بالتقليد الردي عقولُهم، وهم أحبار اليهود والمنافقون والمشركون، فقال:","quran_ar":"قُلْ أَتُحَاجُّونَنا فِي اللَّهِ وَهُوَ رَبُّنا وَرَبُّكُمْ وَلَنا أَعْمالُنا وَلَكُمْ أَعْمالُكُمْ وَنَحْنُ لَهُ مُخْلِصُونَ (139) أَمْ تَقُولُونَ إِنَّ إِبْراهِيمَ وَإِسْماعِيلَ وَإِسْحاقَ وَيَعْقُوبَ وَالْأَسْباطَ كانُوا هُوداً أَوْ نَصارى قُلْ أَأَنْتُمْ أَعْلَمُ أَمِ اللَّهُ وَمَنْ أَظْلَمُ مِمَّنْ كَتَمَ شَهادَةً عِنْدَهُ مِنَ اللَّهِ وَمَا اللَّهُ بِغافِلٍ عَمَّا تَعْمَلُونَ (140) تِلْكَ أُمَّةٌ قَدْ خَلَتْ لَها ما كَسَبَتْ وَلَكُمْ ما كَسَبْتُمْ وَلا تُسْئَلُونَ عَمَّا كانُوا يَعْمَلُونَ (141)","quran_en":"139. Say, “Do you dispute with us concerning God, although He is our Lord and your Lord? We have our deeds and you have yours; to Him we are sincere.” 140. Or do you say that Abraham, Ishmael, Isaac, Jacob and the Tribes were Jews or Christians? Say, “Do you know better, or does God?” Who is more unjust than one who conceals testimony received from God? God is not unaware of what you do. 141. That was a community which has passed away. Theirs is what they earned, and yours what you earned. You will not be questioned about what they used to do.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Disapproval of both claims","html":"<p>I say: <i>am<\/i> appears to be disconnected, meaning “rather,” whether the verb is read in the second or third person. The intention is to reject both things, not merely one of them.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: am appears to be disconnected, meaning “rather,” whether the verb is read in the second or third person. The intention is to reject both things, not merely one of them."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Neither precedence nor ancestry secures a monopoly","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: Muḥammad, ask the People of the Book whether they dispute concerning God, claiming greater entitlement to Him, when He is our Lord and yours, not restricted to one rather than another. We have deeds by which we draw near, and you have yours, by which you too draw near. How can you monopolize proximity when we are sincere to Him in deeds and hearts, whereas you associate another with Him? If you claim all prophets belonged to you and your religion, you have spoken falsely. Do you claim that Abraham, Ishmael, Isaac, Jacob and his sons, the Tribes, were Jews according to your religion, Jewish people, or Christians according to yours, Christian people?<\/p><p>Ask them: do you know better, or God? He denied both of Abraham: “Abraham was neither Jew nor Christian, but upright in surrender” [3:67]; “The Torah and Gospel were sent down only after him” [3:65]. Those mentioned with him followed his religion, so were neither Jews nor Christians. How can you claim them all for your religion when you bear witness otherwise? None is more unjust than one concealing God’s testimony<\/p><p>that Abraham was upright and free of Judaism and Christianity. God is not unaware of your acts: He requites even the tiniest thing. Reliance on descent from them is delusion.<\/p><p>That community has passed. Its deeds belong to it and do not benefit others; yours belong to you and nothing else benefits you. You are not questioned about their deeds, nor they about yours. Al-Bayḍāwī says the repetition intensifies warning against the deeply rooted habit of boasting in fathers and relying upon them. Another view takes the earlier address as directed to them and this one to us, warning against imitation. Another takes the first “community” as the prophets and the second as the Jewish and Christian ancestors.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: Muḥammad, ask the People of the Book whether they dispute concerning God, claiming greater entitlement to Him, when He is our Lord and yours, not restricted to one rather than another. We have deeds by which we draw near, and you have yours, by which you too draw near. How can you monopolize proximity when we are sincere to Him in deeds and hearts, whereas you associate another with Him? If you claim all prophets belonged to you and your religion, you have spoken falsely. Do you claim that Abraham, Ishmael, Isaac, Jacob and his sons, the Tribes, were Jews according to your religion, Jewish people, or Christians according to yours, Christian people? Ask them: do you know better, or God? He denied both of Abraham: “Abraham was neither Jew nor Christian, but upright in surrender” [3:67]; “The Torah and Gospel were sent down only after him” [3:65]. Those mentioned with him followed his religion, so were neither Jews nor Christians. How can you claim them all for your religion when you bear witness otherwise? None is more unjust than one concealing God’s testimony that Abraham was upright and free of Judaism and Christianity. God is not unaware of your acts: He requites even the tiniest thing. Reliance on descent from them is delusion. That community has passed. Its deeds belong to it and do not benefit others; yours belong to you and nothing else benefits you. You are not questioned about their deeds, nor they about yours. Al-Bayḍāwī says the repetition intensifies warning against the deeply rooted habit of boasting in fathers and relying upon them. Another view takes the earlier address as directed to them and this one to us, warning against imitation. Another takes the first “community” as the prophets and the second as the Jewish and Christian ancestors."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The equality of callings before the criterion of sincerity","html":"<p>Whoever the Real establishes in an occupation and directs towards it works for God there and fulfils God’s purpose in it. Works differ through intentions; people differ in degree through sincerity. All creatures are servants of the Glorious King. Special distinction arises only through sincerity: whoever is more sincere to God is nearer to Him than another. In proportion to the servant’s purity comes his election.<\/p><p>Sufis, scholars, worshippers, renunciants and those engaged in worldly occupations of every kind all work for God. None has greater entitlement to Him than another except through sincerity and the heart’s exclusive devotion to Him. Whoever claims distinction with God on any other basis is lying. Whoever relies on another’s work is deluded and is told: “That community has passed; theirs is what they earned, yours what you earned; you will not be questioned about what they did.”<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Whoever the Real establishes in an occupation and directs towards it works for God there and fulfils God’s purpose in it. Works differ through intentions; people differ in degree through sincerity. All creatures are servants of the Glorious King. Special distinction arises only through sincerity: whoever is more sincere to God is nearer to Him than another. In proportion to the servant’s purity comes his election. Sufis, scholars, worshippers, renunciants and those engaged in worldly occupations of every kind all work for God. None has greater entitlement to Him than another except through sincerity and the heart’s exclusive devotion to Him. Whoever claims distinction with God on any other basis is lying. Whoever relies on another’s work is deluded and is told: “That community has passed; theirs is what they earned, yours what you earned; you will not be questioned about what they did.”"},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>When God wished to change the prayer-direction from Syria towards the Kaʿba, He announced that people of light judgement and minds corrupted by bad imitation—the Jewish learned men, hypocrites and polytheists—would object, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"When God wished to change the prayer-direction from Syria towards the Kaʿba, He announced that people of light judgement and minds corrupted by bad imitation—the Jewish learned men, hypocrites and polytheists—would object, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":3128,"arabic_characters":3084}];
